const sources=[{"title": "-PAR- 11 Trypanosomes and Leishmania.pdf", "pages": ["PARASITOLOGY \nLeishmania and Trypanosoma \nDr. Lourdes Trinidad M. Dominguez  | October 29, 2025 | A.Y. 2025-2026 \n \n \nTABLE OF CONTENTS \n \n1. HEMOFLAGELLATES\u200b\n1 \n1.1. LEISHMANIA SPP.\u200b\n2 \n1.1.1. EPIDEMIOLOGY\u200b\n2 \n1.1.2. MORPHOLOGY\u200b\n2 \n1.1.3. LIFE CYCLE\u200b\n2 \n1.1.4. CLINICAL FEATURES\u200b\n3 \n1.1.5. LABORATORY DIAGNOSIS\u200b\n5 \n1.1.5.1. LEISHMANIA SKIN TEST (MONTENEGRO \nTEST)\u200b\n6 \n1.1.5.2. OTHER TESTS\u200b\n6 \n1.1.6. TREATMENT\u200b\n6 \n1.1.7. PREVENTION AND CONTROL\u200b\n6 \n1.2. TRYPANOSOMA CRUZI\u200b\n6 \n1.2.1. EPIDEMIOLOGY\u200b\n6 \n1.2.2. MORPHOLOGY\u200b\n7 \n1.2.3. LIFE CYCLE\u200b\n7 \n1.2.4. CLINICAL FEATURES\u200b\n8 \n1.2.5. LABORATORY DIAGNOSIS\u200b\n9 \n1.2.6. TREATMENT\u200b\n9 \n1.2.7. PREVENTION AND CONTROL\u200b\n10 \n1.3. TRYPANOSOMA BRUCEI GAMBIENSE\u200b\n10 \n1.3.1. EPIDEMIOLOGY\u200b\n10 \n1.3.2. MORPHOLOGY\u200b\n10 \n1.3.3. LIFE CYCLE\u200b\n11 \n1.3.4. CLINICAL FEATURES\u200b\n12 \n1.3.5. LABORATORY DIAGNOSIS\u200b\n13 \n1.3.6. TREATMENT\u200b\n13 \n1.3.7. PREVENTION AND CONTROL\u200b\n13 \n1.4. TRYPANOSOMA BRUCEI RHODESIENSE\u200b\n13 \n1.4.1. EPIDEMIOLOGY\u200b\n13 \n1.4.2. CLINICAL FEATURES\u200b\n14 \n1.4.3. LABORATORY DIAGNOSIS\u200b\n14 \n1.4.4. TREATMENT\u200b\n14 \n1.4.5. PREVENTION & CONTROL\u200b\n14 \n \n \n \nLEGEND \n\ud83d\udcd7\u200b Reference Book \n\ud83d\udcd6\u200b Previous Trans \n\u2605\u200b Take Note / Important Info \n \n1. HEMOFLAGELLATES  \n\u25cf\u200b\nFlagellated protozoans that are found in the peripheral blood \ncirculation \n\u25cf\u200b\nInvolves 2 hosts for the completion of their life cycle \n\u25cb\u200b\nVertebrate host \n\u25cb\u200b\nInsect vector \n\u25cf\u200b\nFour (4) morphological states for life cycle completion: \n\u25cb\u200b\nAmastigote \n\u25cb\u200b\nPromastigote \n\u25cb\u200b\nEpimastigote \n\u25cb\u200b\nTrypomastigote \n\ud83d\udcd6\u200b All forms are seen in Trypanosoma cruzi infections \n\ud83d\udcd6\u200b Only epimastigote and trypomastigote are seen in \nTrypanosoma brucei infections. \n\ud83d\udcd6\u200b Only amastigote and promastigote are seen in Leishmania \ninfections \n \nFig 1.  Morphological Stages of Hemoflagellates.  \nFrom left to right : amastigote,promastigote, epimastigote, and \ntrypomastigote \n(Lecture 9 Leishmania, Trypanosoma, Coccidian Protozoa 2023) \n \nMORPHOLOGICAL STAGES OF HEMOFLAGELLATES \nAmastigote \n \nFound intracellularly (primarily \nin muscle, tissue, CNS within \nmacrophage where they \nmultiply) \nPromastigote \n \nMay only be seen if blood \nsample is collected immediately \nafter transmission \nEpimastigote \n \nFound primarily in arthropod \nvector \nTrypomastigote \n \nAble to reproduce; visible in \nperipheral blood \n \n1.1. LEISHMANIA SPP. \n\u25cf\u200b\n2 subgenera: \n\u25cb\u200b\nL. leishmania \n\u25cb\u200b\nL. vianna \n\u25cf\u200b\nCan be differentiated based on: \n\u25cb\u200b\nLocation of endemicity \n\u25cb\u200b\nLocation of development inside the insect \n\u25cf\u200b\nReservoir: \n\u25cb\u200b\nHumans \n\u25cb\u200b\nDogs \n\u25cb\u200b\nRodents \n \n1.1.1. EPIDEMIOLOGY \n\u25cf\u200b\nIt causes leishmaniasis \nTYPES OF LEISHMANIA \n \nOLD WORLD \nLEISHMANIASIS \nNEW WORLD \nLEISHMANIASIS \n \n 1 \n", "PARASITOLOGY | TRANS TOPIC \nLocation \nAsia, Africa, Eastern \nEurope \nMexico, Central and \nSouth America \nVector \nSandfly \n(genus Phlebotomus) \nSandfly \n(genus Lutzomyia) \nSubspecies \nL.L donovani \nL.L infantum \nL.L tropica \nL.L major \nL.L chagasi \nL.L mexicana complex \nL.V braziliensis complex \nL.L amazonensis \n \n \nFig 2.  . Leishmania vector \n(Lecture 9 Leishmania, Trypanosoma, Coccidian Protozoa 2023) \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n1.1.2. MORPHOLOGY \n \nAMASTIGOTE \n\u25cf\u200b\nIntracellular parasites \n\u25cf\u200b\nProduced and develops inside mammalian host \n\u25cf\u200b\nFound in tissues \n\u25cf\u200b\nLives intracellularly in monocytes, PMNs, or endothelial cells \nCharacteristics \n\u25cf\u200b\nSmall (3-5 um in diameter) \n\u25cf\u200b\nOvoid, non-motile, intracellular forms \n\u25cf\u200b\nNucleus is large \n\u25cf\u200b\nAxoneme arises from kinetoplast and \nextends to anterior tip \nPROMASTIGOTE \n\u25cf\u200b\nDepending on the genus of the vector, it develops in a specific \nbody part of the insect vector \nHindgut \nL. vianna \nMidgut \nL. vianna \nL. leishmania \nProboscis \nCharacteristics \n\u25cf\u200b\nElongated \n\u25cf\u200b\nMotile, extracellular stages (1.5-3.5 by \n15-20 um) \n\u25cf\u200b\nSingle free flagellum (15-28 um long) \n \n\u0000 AMASTIGOTES: Intracellular \nPROMASTIGOTES: Extracellular w/ free flagellum \n \n1.1.3. LIFE CYCLE \n \n \n1 \nSandfly takes a blood meal, injecting the promastigote into \nthe mammalian host. The promastigote in the proboscis of \nthe sandfly will be injected into the mammalian host \n2 \nThe promastigote is phagocytosed by the macrophages or \nother cells of the reticuloendothelial system (RES). \n3 \nInside the macrophages, the promastigote transforms into \namastigote. Eventually, the cell that houses the amastigotes \nwill rupture and release it to the circulation. \n4 \nThe amastigotes will be released in the circulation and can \ninfect other cells in the host. Once a sandfly does another \nblood meal, it can take up these amastigotes and the life \ncycle of development continues in the insect vector. \n5 \nThe amastigote develops into promastigote in the gut (of the \ninsect) \n6 \nPromastigotes eventually migrate to the proboscis, repeating \nthe cycle. \n \nINFECTIVE STAGE \nPromastigote (Proboscis of insect) \nDIAGNOSTIC STAGE \nAmastigote (found in the tissues) \nMODE OF \nTRANSMISSION \n\u25cf\u200b\nBite wounds \n\u25cf\u200b\nBlood transfusion \n\u25cf\u200b\nDirect contact with contaminated \nspecimens \n\u25cf\u200b\nVertical \ntransmission \n(Mother\u2192 \nbaby) \nRESERVOIR \nRodents, Dogs, other mammals \nVECTOR \nSandfly \nDEFINITIVE HOST \nHumans \nLOCATION IN MAN \nReticuloendothelial System (RES) \n \n1.1.4. CLINICAL FEATURES \n\u25cf\u200b\nThere are 4 common forms of Leishmaniasis \n\u25cb\u200b\nCutaneous leishmaniasis (CL) \n\u25cb\u200b\nDiffuse cutaneous leishmaniasis (DCL) \n\u25cb\u200b\nMucocutaneous leishmaniasis (MCL) \n\u25cb\u200b\nVisceral leishmaniasis \n \n \n \nLet's capy-talize on our learning with these awesome notes!  |  2 \n", "PARASITOLOGY | TRANS TOPIC \n \nLEISHMANIASIS \nCHARACTERISTICS \nSPECIES CAUSING \nINFECTION \nCLINICAL PRESENTATION \nCUTANEOUS \n\u25cf\u200b\nThe \nmost \ncommon form of \nleishmaniasis \n\u25cf\u200b\n\u201cOriental button\u201d - erythematous \npapule \nor \nnodule \nat \nthe \ninoculation site (where the insect \nhas bitten) \n\u25cb\u200b\nRaised edges and a central \ncrater \n\u25cb\u200b\nBecomes a violaceous ulcer \n\u25cb\u200b\nHeals \nspontaneously \n\u2192 \nleaves a disfiguring scar \n\u25cf\u200b\nMay progress to other forms of \nleishmaniasis \n \n \nDIFFUSE \nCUTANEOUS \n\u25cf\u200b\nAnergic or lepromatous \nleishmaniasis \n\u25cf\u200b\nRare \n\u25cf\u200b\nLocalized, non-ulcerating papule \n\u25cf\u200b\nForms diffuse satellite lesions \n\u25cf\u200b\nAffects face and extremities \n\u25cf\u200b\nMay be mistaken as \nlepromatous leprosy \n\u25cf\u200b\nNo cell-mediated immunity (CMI) \nto parasites \n\u25cf\u200b\nL. aethiopica \n\u25cf\u200b\nL. mexicana \n \nMUCOCUTANE\nOUS \n\u25cf\u200b\nInvolves mucous membranes of \nnasal and oral cavities  \n\u25cf\u200b\nOccurs concurrently with or years \nafter resolution of skin lesions  \n\u25cf\u200b\nSpread from cutaneous \nleishmaniasis  \n\u25cf\u200b\nPresents with nasal discharge or \nstuffiness, epistaxis, destruction \nof nasal septum (\u201cespundia\u201d)  \n\u25cf\u200b\nInvolvement of the pharynx &  \nlarynx leads to dysphonia and \ndysphagia  \n\u25cf\u200b\nL. braziliensis \n\u25cf\u200b\nL. tropica \n \n \n \nLet's capy-talize on our learning with these awesome notes!  |  3 \n", "PARASITOLOGY | TRANS TOPIC \nVISCERAL \n(KALA AZAR) \n\u25cf\u200b\nCan be asymptomatic \n\u25cf\u200b\nAcute phase \n\u25cb\u200b\nFever \n\u25cb\u200b\nChills \n\u25a0\u200b\nCan be mistaken as \nMalaria since the fever \ncan occur twice daily \n(double quotidian) \n\u25cf\u200b\nSubacute and chronic phase \n\u25cb\u200b\nFever \n\u25cb\u200b\nWeakness \n\u25cb\u200b\nAnorexia \n\u25cb\u200b\nWeight loss \n\u25cb\u200b\nAbdominal enlargement \n\u25a0\u200b\nHepatosplenomegaly is \nusually seen \n\u25cf\u200b\nDarkening of the skin \n\u25cf\u200b\nFew phagocytosed amastigotes \nin the blood but numerous in \nreticuloendothelial \ncells \n\u2192 \nspleen, liver, lymph nodes, bone \nmarrow, and other organs \n\u25cb\u200b\nHence, visceral because it is \nseen in the organs \n\u25cf\u200b\nL. \ndonovani \ncomplex: \n\u25cb\u200b\nL. \ndonovani \n\u25cb\u200b\nL. \ninfantum \n\u25cb\u200b\nL. chagasi \n \nPOST-KALA AZAR DERMAL LEISHMANIASIS (PKDL) \n\u25cf\u200b\nSequelae of VL \n\u25cf\u200b\nCutaneous \neruption \nresulting \nto \nhypopigmented \nmacules, malar erythema, nodules, ulcerations \n\u25cf\u200b\nManifests months to years after treatment of VL \n \n \n \n1.1.5. LABORATORY DIAGNOSIS \n\u25cf\u200b\nDefinitive \ndiagnosis \ndepends \non \ndemonstrating \nthe \namastigotes in tissue or promastigotes in culture \n\u25cb\u200b\nCulture: Novy, MacNeal, Nicolle (NNN) medium or \nScheider\u2019s medium \n\u25cf\u200b\nSkin scraping for biopsy can be done for cutaneous lesions \n\u25cf\u200b\nSpleen, bone marrow, or liver aspirates for visceral \nleishmaniasis \n \n \nFig 3..Leishman-Donovan bodies or LD bodies - macrophages that contain \nAmastigotes.  Amastigotes in the tissues with ovoid structures in the cell \n(Lecture 9 Leishmania, Trypanosoma, Coccidian Protozoa 2023 \n \n \n \nFig 4. Promastigotes. Seen in culture (flagellated type) \n(Lecture 9 Leishmania, Trypanosoma, Coccidian Protozoa 2023 \n \n1.1.5.1. LEISHMANIA SKIN TEST (MONTENEGRO TEST) \n\u25cf\u200b\nInvolves hypersensitivity test reaction to a suspension of killed \npromastigotes \n\u25cb\u200b\nDelayed hypersensitivity type of reaction \n\u25cf\u200b\nUsed to identify exposure to the parasites \n \nPOSITIVE REACTION \n\u2265 5 mm in 72 hours \nCL and MCL \nNEGATIVE REACTION \nDCL and VL/KL \n \n \n \nLet's capy-talize on our learning with these awesome notes!  |  4 \n", "PARASITOLOGY | TRANS TOPIC \n \nFig 5. Montenegro Test. \n(Lecture 9 Leishmania, Trypanosoma, Coccidian Protozoa 2023) \n \n1.1.5.2. OTHER TESTS \n\u25cf\u200b\nAntibody detection in serum \u2014 ELISA, DAT \n\u25cf\u200b\nMolecular method \u2014 PCR \n\u25cf\u200b\nAnimal inoculation \u2014 intranasal inoculation to golden hamster \n\u25cb\u200b\nCan detect low intensity of infection \n \n1.1.6. TREATMENT \n\u25cf\u200b\nSodium stibogluconate and meglumine antimoniate \n\u25cf\u200b\nLiposomal amphotericin B \n \n1.1.7. PREVENTION AND CONTROL \n\u25cf\u200b\nVector control \n\u25cf\u200b\nApplication of insect repellents to the skin \n\u25cf\u200b\nUse of personal protective equipment when traveling to \nendemic areas \n\u25cf\u200b\nEarly detection and treatment of cases \n\u25cf\u200b\nControl of dogs or rodent reservoirs \n \n1.2. TRYPANOSOMA CRUZI \n \n1.2.1. EPIDEMIOLOGY \n\u25cf\u200b\nCauses \nChaga\u2019s \nDisease \nor \nSouth \nAmerican \ntrypanosomiasis  \n\u25cf\u200b\nLimited to South and Central America  \n\u25cf\u200b\nZoonotic  \n\u25cf\u200b\nReservoirs  \n\u25cb\u200b\nDogs, \ncats, pigs, opossums, armadillos, racoons, \nmarsupials, and rodents  \n\u25cf\u200b\nVector: reduviid bugs or kissing bugs or triatomine bugs  \n\u25cb\u200b\nTriatoma  \n\u25cb\u200b\nRhodnius  \n\u25cb\u200b\nPanstrongylus \n \n \nFig 6. T.cruzi vector  \n(Lecture 9 Leishmania, Trypanosoma, Coccidian Protozoa 2023) \n \n \n \n \n \n \n1.2.2. MORPHOLOGY \n \n \nFig 7. T.cruzi (A) trypomastigote and (B) amastigote form \n(Lecture 9 Leishmania, Trypanosoma, Coccidian Protozoa 2023) \n \n4 STAGES OF DEVELOPMENT \namastigote, epimastigote, promastigote, trypomastigote \nVertebrate \nhost \nTrypomastigote \nFound in the blood \nAmastigote \nFound in tissues \nInsect host \nAmastigote \nEpimastigote \nPromastigote \nDevelops in the midgut \nof the insect \nTrypomastigote \nDevelops in the hindgut \nof the insect \n \n \n1.2.3. LIFE CYCLE \n \n1 \nThe insect takes a blood meal from the host and will \ndefecate after. This allows the parasite in the feces to enter \nvia bite site. The parasite can also enter via intact mucus \nmembranes like conjunctiva. \n\u25cf\u200b\n Enters the host as metacyclic trypomastigotes and \ninfects cells near the inoculation site \n2 \nOnce entered, it can infect cells near the bite site. \nMetacyclic trypomastigote inside the cell will eventually \ndevelop into amastigote. \n3 \nAfter undergoing binary fission, amastigote will develop into \ntrypomastigote.  \n4 \nTrypomastigotes will be released in the circulation and can \ninfect other cells which will develop again into amastigotes \nthat will eventually become trypomastigotes. \n5 \nOnce a vector takes a blood meal, it can carry the \ntrypomastigote from the host. The cycle continues in the \nbody of insect.  \n \n \nLet's capy-talize on our learning with these awesome notes!  |  5 \n", "PARASITOLOGY | TRANS TOPIC \n6 \nEpimastigote will develop in the midgut and go into the \nhindgut to become metacyclic trypomastigote which will be \nreleased in the feces of the insect vector \n \nINFECTIVE STAGE \nMetacyclic trypomastigote \n(released in the feces of insect) \nDIAGNOSTIC STAGE \nTrypomastigote \n(seen in blood of patient) \nMODE OF \nTRANSMISSION \n\u25cf\u200b\nFecal contamination of mucus \nmembranes, conjunctiva, or \nwound on skin surface \n\u25cf\u200b\nBlood transfusion \n\u25cf\u200b\nOrgan transplantation \n\u25cf\u200b\nVertical transmission \nBUG STAGES \n\u25cf\u200b\nParasite develops in the hindgut \nof the vector to become \nmetacyclic trypomastigotes \u2192 \ncarried in the feces of the insect \n\u25cf\u200b\nUnique feature: defecates when \nit takes a blood meal \n \n1.2.4. CLINICAL FEATURES \n \nACUTE STAGE \n\u25cf\u200b\nFever or swelling around the site of inoculation \n\u25cf\u200b\nROMA\u00d1A\u2019S SIGN \n\u25cb\u200b\nSwelling of eyelid \n\u25cb\u200b\nNon-pitting edema may develop in other parts of the \nbody but more common in the eyelid \n\u25cf\u200b\nCHAGOMA \n\u25cb\u200b\nErythematous subcutaneous nodule at the bite site \n\u25cb\u200b\nRepresents the site of entry of the parasite \n\u25cf\u200b\nGeneralized lymphadenopathy and hepatosplenomegaly \n\u25cb\u200b\nAmastigote can be seen from aspirated fluid from \nlymph and nodules (since these are tissues). \nTrypomastigote can also be seen sometimes. \n\u25cf\u200b\nMyocarditis and neurologic signs like meningoencephalitis \n \nFig 8. Roma\u00f1a\u2019s sign (left) and Chagoma (right) \n(Lecture 9 Leishmania, Trypanosoma, Coccidian Protozoa 2023) \nINDETERMINATE STAGE \n\u25cf\u200b\n8 to 10 weeks after infection \n\u25cf\u200b\nPatients do not have any symptoms \nCHRONIC STAGE \n\u25cf\u200b\nCardiomyopathy \n\u25cf\u200b\nMegacolon \n\u25cf\u200b\nMegaesophagus \n \n1.2.5. LABORATORY DIAGNOSIS \n\u25cf\u200b\nComplete history for possible exposure  \n\u25cb\u200b\nTravel history  \n\u25cf\u200b\nThick and thin blood smear  \n\u25cb\u200b\nView parasite in the blood (trypomastigote stage)  \n\u25cf\u200b\nMicrohematocrit concentration  \n\u25cb\u200b\nFor low parasitemia  \n\u25cf\u200b\nSerology - complement fixation (specific and practical), EIA, \nIFA, RIA  \n\u25cf\u200b\nXenodiagnosis  \n\u25cb\u200b\nTrypanosome-free bugs in a container are allowed to \nfeed on individuals suspected of Chagas\u2019 disease  \n\u25cb\u200b\nIf organisms are present in the blood meal, the parasites \nmultiply and can be detected in the bug\u2019s intestinal \ncontents  \n\u25cb\u200b\nExamined \nfor \npresence \nof \nT. \ncruzi \nmetacyclic \ntrypomastigotes \n \n \nFig 9. Xenodiagnosis \n(Lecture 9 Leishmania, Trypanosoma, Coccidian Protozoa 2023) \n \n\u25cf\u200b\nBlood culture: Novy, McNeal, & Nicolle (NNN)  \n\u25cf\u200b\nPCR  \n\u25cf\u200b\nExcision biopsy of chagomas  \n\u25cb\u200b\nAmastigote present in tissue \n \n \n \nFig 10. Morphologic stages of parasite in the host \n(Lecture 9 Leishmania, Trypanosoma, Coccidian Protozoa 2023) \n \n\u25cf\u200b\nTrypomastigote (left pic): long and slender or short and \nstumpy forms: C, U, S-shaped with prominent kinetoplast and \nsingle thread-like flagellum  \n\u25cf\u200b\nAmastigote (center): round or ovoid; in groups of cyst like \ncollections inside tissues  \n\u25cf\u200b\nTrypomastigote (right): IFA test \n \n\u2605 AMASTIGOTES - inside the cells or tissues \nTRYPOMASTIGOTES - blood \n \n1.2.6. TREATMENT \n\u25cf\u200b\nDOC: Nifurtimox and Benznidazole  \n\u25cf\u200b\nAllopurinol and itraconazole  \n\u25cb\u200b\nShown to halt progression to cardiomyopathy  \n\u25cf\u200b\nSymptomatic and supportive treatment  \n\u25cf\u200b\nSurgery - for the mega syndromes  \n \n1.2.7. PREVENTION AND CONTROL \n\u25cf\u200b\nSpraying insecticide inside the housing to eliminate triatomine \nbugs  \n\u25cf\u200b\nImprove housing/living conditions to eliminate breeding places \nof bugs  \n\u25cf\u200b\nScreening of blood donations for Chagas  \n\u25cf\u200b\nEarly detection and treatment of new cases, including \nmother-to-baby (congenital) cases  \n \n \nLet's capy-talize on our learning with these awesome notes!  |  6 \n", "PARASITOLOGY | TRANS TOPIC \n \n1.3. TRYPANOSOMA BRUCEI GAMBIENSE \n\u25cf\u200b\nPart of the Trypanosoma brucei complex along with T. b. \nrhodesiense \n \n1.3.1. EPIDEMIOLOGY \n\u25cf\u200b\nCauses West African / Gambian / Chronic sleeping sickness \n\u25cf\u200b\nAccounts for 95% of all Human African Trypanosomiasis or \nHAT cases \n\u25cf\u200b\nLimited and common to tropical West and Central Africa \n\u25cf\u200b\nReservoir hosts: dogs, pigs, and sheep \n\u25cf\u200b\nVector and intermediate host: \n\u25cb\u200b\nGlossina flies (Tsetse fly) \n\u25a0\u200b\nMembers: \n\u25cf\u200b\nGlossina palpalis \n\u25cf\u200b\nGlossina tachynoides \n\u25a0\u200b\ncan be transmitted by both males and females \n\u25cf\u200b\nIn some infections/diseases, only the females \ncan infect the mammalian host, such as in \nMalaria or Dengue. \n \n \n \n \nFig 11. The insect vector of T. b. gambiense: the Tsetse fly \n(Lecture 9 Leishmania, Trypanosoma, Coccidian Protozoa 2023) \n \n1.3.2. MORPHOLOGY \n\u25cf\u200b\nNeeds 2 hosts for its life cycle to continue \n \nMORPHOLOGICAL STAGES OF T. B. GAMBIENSE \nVertebrate \nhost stages \n(1) Trypomastigote \nLong, slender \nform \n \nShort, stumpy \nform \nintermediate \nforms \n \nInsect host \n(1) Epimastigote \n \n(2) Procyclic trypomastigote \n \n(3) Metacyclic trypomastigote \n \n \n \n \n1.3.3. LIFE CYCLE \n \n \n1 \nDuring a blood meal on the mammalian host, an infected \ntsetse fly injects metacyclic trypomastigotes (infective \nstage to mammalian host) into skin tissue. The parasites \nenter the lymphatic system and pass into the bloodstream. \n2 \nInside the host, they transform into bloodstream \ntrypomastigotes. \n3 \nThey are arried to other sites throughout the body, reach \nother body fluids (e.g., blood, lymph, spinal fluid), and \ncontinue the replication by binary fission. \n4 \nCirculating blood trypomastigotes (diagnostic stage) \nduring acute phase; usually undetectable in latent phase. \n5 \nThe Tsetse fly becomes infected with bloodstream \ntrypomastigotes (infective stage to insect vector) through \ningestion when taking a blood meal on an infected \nmammalian host. \n6 \nIn the fly\u2019s midgut, the parasites transform into procyclic \ntrypomastigotes, which multiply by binary fission. \n7 \nThey leave the midgut and then transform into \nepimastigotes. \n8 \nThe epimastigotes reach the fly\u2019s salivary glands and \ncontinue multiplication by binary fission. They move to the \nproboscis and transform into metacyclic trypomastigotes. \n \nLIFE CYCLE KEY POINTS \nINFECTIVE STAGE \n(to mammalian host) \nMetacyclic trypomastigotes \nDIAGNOSTIC \nSTAGE \n(Infective stage to \nvector host) \nTrypomastigote \nMODE OF \nTRANSMISSION \n\u25cf\u200b\nBite of infected tsetse fly and other \nblood-sucking insects \n\u25cf\u200b\nAccidental needle pricks \n\u25cb\u200b\nExtracting \nblood \nfrom \nan \ninfected person and you get a \nneedle prick \n\u25cf\u200b\nVertical / congenital transmission \n\u25cb\u200b\nFrom mother to baby \n \n \nLet's capy-talize on our learning with these awesome notes!  |  7 \n", "PARASITOLOGY | TRANS TOPIC \n \n1.3.4. CLINICAL FEATURES \n\u25cf\u200b\nFollows a chronic course that progresses slowly \n\u25cf\u200b\nInitial lesion: localized, painful, pruritic, erythematous chancre \nat bite site that will eventually disappear \u2192 forms a central \neschar \n\u25cf\u200b\nResolves spontaneously after 2-3 weeks \n \nFig 12. Initial lesion caused by T. b. gambiense infection (chancre) \n(Lecture 9 Leishmania, Trypanosoma, Coccidian Protozoa 2023) \n \nMORPHOLOGICAL STAGES OF T. B. GAMBIENSE \nEARLY PHASE \nLATE PHASE \n\u25cf\u200b\nParasites can be seen in: \n\u25cb\u200b\nthe bloodstream \n\u25a0\u200b\nespecially in \nfebrile episodes \n\u25cb\u200b\nthe lymphatics \n\u25cf\u200b\nFevers, headaches, muscle \nand joint aches, malaise \n\u25cf\u200b\nItching of the skin, swollen \nlymph nodes, and weight \nloss \n\u25cf\u200b\nWinterbottom\u2019s Sign \n\u25cb\u200b\nEnlarged, non-tender \nLN / lymphadenopathy \n(commonly seen in the \nposterior cervical, \naxillary, and \nsupraclavicular LN) \n\u25cf\u200b\nMeningoencephalic phase \n\u25cf\u200b\nCNS involvement, with \nbehavioral changes, \nheadache, daytime \nsleepiness with nighttime \nsleep disturbance, \nprogressive confusion, and \nspeech defects  \n\u25cf\u200b\nKerandel\u2019s Sign \n\u25cb\u200b\nDelayed sensation to \npain \n\u25cf\u200b\nSomnolence (strong \ndesire for sleep) \u2192 deep \ncoma \u2192 death \n\u25cb\u200b\nHence, its name, \nGambian Sleeping \nSickness \n \n \nFig 13. Winterbottom\u2019s Sign in Early Phase T. brucei gambiense Infection \n(Lecture 9 Leishmania, Trypanosoma, Coccidian Protozoa 2023) \n \n\u25cf\u200b\nAntigenic Variation \n\u25cb\u200b\nTrypanosomes can have a continuous change in the \nsurface coat antigens by turning on or off the genes \ncoding for their Variant Surface Glycoproteins (VSGs) \n\u25cb\u200b\nEvolves every ~ 5-10 days \n\u25cb\u200b\nKey mechanism for evading host immune response \n \n1.3.5. LABORATORY DIAGNOSIS \n\u25cf\u200b\nIdentification of parasite in body fluid or tissue \n\u25cb\u200b\nBlood: \n\u25a0\u200b\nPeripheral Blood Smear (PBS) \n\u25a0\u200b\nThick and thin smear (best done during febrile \nepisodes since more parasites are released during \nthis period) \n\u25cb\u200b\nLymph node fluid (aspiration) \n\u25cb\u200b\nCSF \n\u25cb\u200b\nBiopsy or aspiration of chancre \n\u25cf\u200b\nBuffy coat concentration (microhematocrit concentration \ntechniques) \n\u25cb\u200b\nif there is low parasitemia \n\u25cf\u200b\nSerologic tests \n\u25cb\u200b\nELISA, \nImmunofluorescence \n(IF) \nstaining, \nIndirect \nhemagglutination tests (IHA), PCR \n\u25cf\u200b\nWHO criteria for CNS involvement: \n\u25cb\u200b\nIf there is increased protein in CSF (usually an increased \nIgM) \n= \npathognomonic \n/ \nindicative \nfor \nthe \nmeningoencephalic stage of the disease \n\u25cb\u200b\nIncreased CSF WBC count (>5/\u03bcL) \n\u25cf\u200b\nCard Agglutination Trypanosomiasis Test (CATT) \n\u25cb\u200b\nDetects circulating antigens \n\u25cb\u200b\nMore beneficial for field screening in high risk, large \npopulations \n \nFig 14. T. b. gambiense Trypomastigotes (Diagnostic Stage) in PBS: can \nbe seen in long slender form, stumpy short, broad form, and an \nintermediate form \n(Lecture 9 Leishmania, Trypanosoma, Coccidian Protozoa 2023) \n \n1.3.6. TREATMENT \n\u25cf\u200b\nPentamidine \n\u25cf\u200b\nOthers (available at CDC only): \n\u25cb\u200b\nSuramin \n\u25cb\u200b\nMelarsoprol \n\u25cb\u200b\nEflornithine \n\u25cb\u200b\nNifurtimox \n \n1.3.7. PREVENTION AND CONTROL \n\u25cf\u200b\nEarly diagnosis and early treatment of cases \n\u25cb\u200b\nHistory (e.g. record of travel history) \n\u25cb\u200b\nPhysical examination \n\u25cf\u200b\nMinimizing contact with tsetse flies (Vector control) \n\u25cb\u200b\ne.g. use of Insect repellents, insecticides, PPEs \n\u25cb\u200b\nWearing long-sleeved shirts and pants of medium-weight \nmaterial in neutral colors \n \n \n \nLet's capy-talize on our learning with these awesome notes!  |  8 \n", "PARASITOLOGY | TRANS TOPIC \n1.4. TRYPANOSOMA BRUCEI RHODESIENSE \n \n1.4.1. EPIDEMIOLOGY \n\u25cf\u200b\nCausative agent for East African Sleeping Sickness \n\u25cb\u200b\nAccounts for 5% of Human African Trypanosomiasis \n(HAT) \n\u25cb\u200b\nIn contrast to T. gambiense which causes 95% of HAT \ncases and involves West Africa \n\u25cf\u200b\nNo morphologic difference with T. gambiense; has greater \nmorbidity and mortality \n\u25cf\u200b\nZoonotic infection \n\u25cb\u200b\nReservoir hosts: cattle and wild animals \n\u25cf\u200b\nVector: Glossina morsitans \n \n1.4.2. CLINICAL FEATURES \n\u25cf\u200b\nMore rapid, fulminating disease (more severe) \n\u25cf\u200b\nFever, severe headaches, irritability, extreme fatigue, swollen \nlymph nodes, and aching muscles and joints \n\u25cf\u200b\nProgressive confusion, personality changes, slurred speech, \nseizures, and difficulty in walking and talking \n\u25cf\u200b\nShort incubation period (1-4 weeks) \n\u25cf\u200b\nTrypomastigotes more numerous and appears earlier in blood \n\u25cf\u200b\nLymph node involvement is less pronounced \n\u25cf\u200b\nWinterbottom\u2019s sign may be absent \n \n1.4.3. LABORATORY DIAGNOSIS \n\u25cf\u200b\nIdentification of parasite in body fluid \n\u25cb\u200b\nBlood: PBS, thick and thin smear \n\u25cb\u200b\nLymph node fluid or CSF via aspiration or chancre biopsy \n\u25cf\u200b\nBuffy coat concentration (Microhematocrit concentration) \n\u25cb\u200b\nCan be used if there is low parasitemia \n\u25cf\u200b\nOther tests (serological): ELISA, IF, IHA test, PCR \n\u25cf\u200b\nWHO Criteria for CNS Involvement: \n\u25cb\u200b\nIncreased protein (IgM) in CSF - pathognomonic for the \nmeningoencephalic stage of the disease \n\u25cb\u200b\nWhite cell count of more than 5/uL \n\u25cf\u200b\nCan be inoculated in small animals and cultured in laboratory \n \n1.4.4. TREATMENT \n\u25cf\u200b\nPentamidine \n\u25cf\u200b\nOthers (available at CDC only) \n\u25cb\u200b\nSuramin, Melarsoprol, Eflornithine, and Nifurtimox \n \n1.4.5. PREVENTION & CONTROL \n\u25cf\u200b\nEarly diagnosis and treatment of cases \n\u25cb\u200b\nTravel history to endemic areas \n\u25cf\u200b\nMinimizing contact with tsetse flies and use of insect repellant \n\u25cf\u200b\nWearing long-sleeved shirts and pants of medium weight \nmaterial in neutral colors \n\u25cf\u200b\nUse of PPEs to protect possible human hosts \n \n \nTRYPANOSOMA \nBRUCEI GAMBIENSE \nTRYPANOSOMA \nBRUCEI \nRHODESIENSE \nDisease \nWest African \nSleeping Sickness \nEast African \nSleeping Sickness \n% of HAT Cases \n95% \n5% \nVectors \nTsetse Flies \nGlossina palpalis \nGlossina morsitans \nPrimary Reservoir \nHumans  \nAnimals (antelope \nand cattle) \nHuman Illness \nChronic CNS \ndisease \nAcute CNS disease \n(up to 9 months) \nDuration of Illness \nMonths to years \n< 9 months (before \ndeath occurs) \nLymphadenopath\ny \nFrequent, cervical \n(Winterbottom sign) \nMinimal (axial \nand inguinal) \nParasitemia \nLow \nHigh  \nVirulence \nLess  \nMore  \nMorbidity and \nMortality \nLesser  \nGreater  \nRodent \nInoculation \nNot useful \nDiagnostic \nEpidemiology  \nRural populations \nWorkers in wild \nareas, tourists in \ngame parts, and \nrural populations \nResponse to \nDrugs \nLess resistant \nMore resistant \nMorphology  \nNo morphologic difference \n \nEAST AND WEST AFRICAN COUNTRIES (CDC) \n\u25cf\u200b Tsetse flies are found only in Africa and they live in rural \nenvironments. \n\u25cf\u200b East African trypanosomiasis is found in parts of Eastern \nand Southeastern Africa. More than 95% of cases are \nreported from Uganda, Tanzania, Malawi, and Zambia. \n\u25cf\u200b West African trypanosomiasis can be contracted in parts \nof central Africa and in a few areas of West Africa. Most of \nthe reported cases are found in central Africa (Democratic \nRepublic of Congo, Angola, Sudan, Central African \nRepublic, Chad, and northern Uganda). \n \nTRANS CORRECTION FORM \nFor any corrections found in the final uploaded transes in the drive, \nkindly answer the GForms for Trans Corrections indicated below: \n \nL\n \n \nLink: https://forms.gle/9UpzjQ8Ucy2KZC5Q8  \n \n \nLet's capy-talize on our learning with these awesome notes!  |  9 \n", "PARASITOLOGY | TRANS TOPIC \n \n \n \nSUMMARY TABLES: LEISHMANIA \n \nOLD WORLD LEISHMANIASIS \nNEW WORLD LEISHMANIASIS \nLocation \nAsia, Africa, Eastern \nEurope \nMexico, Central and \nSouth America \nVector \nSandfly \n(genus Phlebotomus) \nSandfly \n(genus Lutzomyia) \nVector Subspecies \nL.L donovani \nL.L infantum \nL.L tropica \nL.L major \nL.L chagasi \nL.L mexicana complex \nL.V braziliensis complex \nL.L amazonensis \nInfective Stage \nPromastigote (Proboscis of insect) \nDiagnostic Stage \nAmastigote (found in the tissues) \nMOT \n\u25cf\u200b\nBite wounds \n\u25cf\u200b\nBlood transfusion \n\u25cf\u200b\nDirect contact with contaminated specimens \n\u25cf\u200b\nVertical transmission (Mother\u2192 baby) \nReservoir \n\u25cf\u200b\nRodents, Dogs, other mammals \nDefinitive host \n\u25cf\u200b\nHumans \nLocation in Humans \n\u25cf\u200b\nReticuloendothelial System \nTreatment \n\u25cf\u200b\nSodium stibogluconate and meglumine antimoniate \n\u25cf\u200b\nLiposomal amphotericin B \nPrevention \n\u25cf\u200b\nVector control \n\u25cf\u200b\nApplication of insect repellents to the skin \n\u25cf\u200b\nUse of personal protective equipment when traveling to endemic areas \n\u25cf\u200b\nEarly detection and treatment of cases \n\u25cf\u200b\nControl of dogs or rodent reservoirs \nAmastigote \nSize \n\u25cf\u200b\nSmall (3-5 um in diameter) \nMorphology \n\u25cf\u200b\nOvoid, non-motile, intracellular forms \n\u25cf\u200b\nNucleus is large \n\u25cf\u200b\nAxoneme arises from kinetoplast and extends to anterior tip \nCharacteristics \n\u25cf\u200b\nIntracellular parasites \n\u25cf\u200b\nProduced and develops inside mammalian host \n\u25cf\u200b\nFound in tissues \n\u25cf\u200b\nLives intracellularly in monocytes, PMNs, or endothelial cells \nPromastigote \nSize \n\u25cf\u200b\nMotile, extracellular stages (1.5-3.5 by 15-20 um) \n\u25cf\u200b\nSingle free flagellum (15-28 um long) \nShape \n\u25cf\u200b\nElongated, Extracellular w/ free flagellum \nLocation \n\u25cf\u200b\nDepending on the genus of the vector, it develops in a specific body part of the insect vector \n \n \nClinical Features \nFEATURES \nINFORMATION \nSPECIES \nCutaneous \nleishmaniasis \n(CL) \n \n\u25cf\u200b\nThe most common form of leishmaniasis \n\u25cf\u200b\n\u201cOriental button\u201d  \n\u25cf\u200b\nMay progress to other forms of leishmaniasis \n \nDiffuse \ncutaneous \nleishmaniasis (DCL) \n\u25cf\u200b\nAnergic or lepromatous leishmaniasis \n\u25cf\u200b\nRare \n\u25cf\u200b\nLocalized, non-ulcerating papule \n\u25cf\u200b\nMay be mistaken as lepromatous leprosy \n\u25cf\u200b\nNo cell-mediated immunity (CMI) to parasites \n\u25cf\u200b\nL. aethiopica \n\u25cf\u200b\nL. mexicana \nMucocutaneous \nleishmaniasis (MCL) \n\u25cf\u200b\nInvolves mucous membranes of nasal and oral cavities  \n\u25cf\u200b\nL. braziliensis \n\u25cf\u200b\nL. tropica \n \n \nLet's capy-talize on our learning with these awesome notes!  |  10 \n", "PARASITOLOGY | TRANS TOPIC \n\u25cf\u200b\nOccurs concurrently with or years after resolution of skin lesions  \n\u25cf\u200b\nSpread from cutaneous leishmaniasis  \n\u25cf\u200b\nPresents with nasal discharge or stuffiness, epistaxis, destruction \nof nasal septum (\u201cespundia\u201d)  \nVisceral \nleishmaniasis \n(Kala Azar) \n\u25cf\u200b\nCan be asymptomatic \n\u25cf\u200b\nAcute phase \n\u25cb\u200b\nFever, Chills \n\u25a0\u200b\nCan be mistaken as Malaria since the fever can occur \ntwice daily (double quotidian) \n\u25cf\u200b\nSubacute and chronic phase \n\u25cb\u200b\nFever \n\u25cb\u200b\nWeakness \n\u25cb\u200b\nAnorexia \n\u25cb\u200b\nWeight loss \n\u25cb\u200b\nAbdominal enlargement \n\u25a0\u200b\nHepatosplenomegaly is usually seen \n\u25cf\u200b\nDarkening of the skin \n\u25cf\u200b\nFew phagocytosed amastigotes in the blood but numerous in \nreticuloendothelial cells \u2192 spleen, liver, lymph nodes, bone \nmarrow, and other organs \n\u25cb\u200b\nHence, visceral because it is seen in the organs \n\u25cf\u200b\nL. donovani complex: \n\u25cb\u200b\nL. donovani \n\u25cb\u200b\nL. infantum \n\u25cb\u200b\nL. chagasi \nPOST-KALA AZAR DERMAL LEISHMANIASIS (PKDL) \n\u25cf\u200b\nSequelae of VL \n\u25cf\u200b\nCutaneous eruption resulting to hypopigmented macules, malar erythema, nodules, ulcerations \n\u25cf\u200b\nManifests months to years after treatment of VL \nTESTS \nDEFINITIVE  \n\u25cf\u200b\nDemonstrating the amastigotes in tissue or promastigotes in culture \n\u25cb\u200b\nCulture: Novy, MacNeal, Nicolle (NNN) medium or Scheider\u2019s medium \nCutaneous lesions \n\u25cf\u200b\nSkin Scraping for biopsy \nVisceral leishmaniasis \n\u25cf\u200b\nSpleen, bone marrow, or liver aspirates  \nLEISHMANIA SKIN TEST (MONTENEGRO \nTEST) \n\u25cf\u200b\nInvolves hypersensitivity test reaction to a suspension of killed promastigotes \n\u25cb\u200b\nDelayed hypersensitivity type of reaction \n\u25cf\u200b\nUsed to identify exposure to the parasites \nOTHER TESTS \n\u25cf\u200b\nAntibody detection in serum \u2014 ELISA, DAT \n\u25cf\u200b\nMolecular method \u2014 PCR \n\u25cf\u200b\nAnimal inoculation \u2014 intranasal inoculation to golden hamster \n\u25cb\u200b\nCan detect low intensity of infection \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \nSUMMARY TABLES: TRYPANOSOMES \n \nT. Cruzi \nT. b. Gambiense \nT. b. rhodesiense \nDisease \n\u25cf\u200b\nChaga\u2019s Disease or South \nAmerican trypanosomiasis \n \n\u25cf\u200b\nCauses West African / Gambian / \nChronic sleeping sickness \n\u25cf\u200b\nAccounts for 95% of all Human \nAfrican Trypanosomiasis or HAT \ncases \n \n\u25cf\u200b\nCausative \nagent \nfor \nEast \nAfrican \nSleeping Sickness \n\u25cb\u200b\nAccounts for 5% of Human African \nTrypanosomiasis (HAT) \n \n\u25cf\u200b\nNo \nmorphologic \ndifference \nwith \nT. \n \n \nLet's capy-talize on our learning with these awesome notes!  |  11 \n", "PARASITOLOGY | TRANS TOPIC \n \ngambiense; has greater morbidity and \nmortality \nInfective Stage \n\u25cf\u200b\nMetacyclic \ntrypomastigote \n(released in the feces of \ninsect) \nMetacyclic trypomastigotes \n- \nDiagnostic Stage \n\u25cf\u200b\nTrypomastigote \n(seen in blood of \npatient) \nTrypomastigote \n- \nMOT \n\u25cf\u200b\nFecal contamination of \nmucus membranes, \nconjunctiva, or wound on \nskin surface \n\u25cf\u200b\nBlood transfusion \n\u25cf\u200b\nOrgan transplantation \n\u25cf\u200b\nVertical transmission \n\u25cf\u200b\nBite of infected tsetse fly and \nother blood-sucking insects \n\u25cf\u200b\nAccidental needle pricks \n\u25cf\u200b\nVertical transmission \n- \nBug Stages \n\u25cf\u200b\nParasite develops in the \nhindgut of the vector to \nbecome metacyclic \ntrypomastigotes \u2192 carried \nin the feces of the insect \n\u25cf\u200b\nUnique feature: defecates \nwhen it takes a blood \nmeal \n- \n- \nReservoir \n\u25cf\u200b\nDogs, \ncats, \npigs, \nopossums, \narmadillos, \nracoons, \nmarsupials, \nand rodents  \n\u25cf\u200b\ndogs, pigs, and sheep \n\u25cb\u200b\ncattle and wild animals \nVector \n\u25cf\u200b\nReduviid \nbugs \nor \nkissing \nbugs \nor \ntriatomine bugs  \n\u25cb\u200b\nTriatoma  \n\u25cb\u200b\nRhodnius  \n\u25cb\u200b\nPanstrongylus \n \n\u25cf\u200b\nGlossina flies (Tsetse fly) \n\u25cb\u200b\nGlossina palpalis \n\u25cb\u200b\nGlossina tachynoides \n\u25cb\u200b\ntransmitted by both \nmales and females \n\u25cf\u200b\nGlossina morsitans \nDevelopment \nVertebrate Host:  \n\u25cf\u200b\nTrypomastigote - blood \n\u25cf\u200b\nAmastigote - tissues \nInvertebrate Host: \n\u25cf\u200b\nMidgut \n\u25cb\u200b\nAmastigote \n\u25cb\u200b\nEpimastigote \n\u25cb\u200b\nPromastigote \n\u25cf\u200b\nHindgut \n\u25cb\u200b\nTrypomastigot\ne \nVertebrate Host:  \n\u25cf\u200b\nTrypomastigote  \nInvertebrate Host: \n\u25cf\u200b\nEpimastigote \n\u25cf\u200b\nProcyclic Trypomastigote  \n\u25cf\u200b\nMetacyclic Trypomastigote  \n- \nFeatures \n\u25cf\u200b\nFever or swelling \naround the site of \ninoculation \n\u25cf\u200b\nROMA\u00d1A\u2019S SIGN \n\u25cf\u200b\nCHAGOMA \n\u25cf\u200b\nGeneralized \nlymphadenopathy and \nhepatosplenomegaly \n\u25cb\u200b\nAmastigote & \nTrypomastigot\ne can be seen \nfrom aspirated \nfluid from \nlymph and \nnodules  \n\u25cf\u200b\nMyocarditis and \nmeningoencephalitisenc\nephalitis) \n\u25cf\u200b\nIndeterminate stage - \n\u25cf\u200b\nChronic \n\u25cf\u200b\nInitial lesion: chancre at bite \nsite \nthat \nwill \neventually \ndisappear \u2192 forms a central \neschar \n\u25cf\u200b\nResolves after 2-3 weeks \n\u25cf\u200b\nEARLY STAGE:  \n\u25cb\u200b\nParasites in \nbloodstream & \nlymphatics \n\u25cb\u200b\nFevers, headaches, \nmuscle and joint \naches, malaise \n\u25cb\u200b\nItching of the skin, \nswollen lymph nodes, \nand weight loss \n\u25cb\u200b\nWinterbottom\u2019s Sign \n\u25cf\u200b\nLATE PHASE \n\u25cb\u200b\nMeningoencephalic \n\u25cf\u200b\nMore rapid, fulminating disease \n(more severe) \n\u25cf\u200b\nFever, severe headaches, irritability, \nextreme \nfatigue, \nswollen \nlymph \nnodes, and aching muscles and joints \n\u25cf\u200b\nProgressive \nconfusion, \npersonality \nchanges, slurred speech, seizures, \nand difficulty in walking and talking \n\u25cf\u200b\nShort incubation period (1-4 weeks) \n\u25cf\u200b\nTrypomastigotes more numerous and \nappears earlier in blood \n\u25cf\u200b\nLymph node involvement is less \npronounced \n\u25cf\u200b\nWinterbottom\u2019s sign may be absent \n \n \nLet's capy-talize on our learning with these awesome notes!  |  12 \n", "PARASITOLOGY | TRANS TOPIC \n8-10 weeks, no \nsymptoms \n\u25cf\u200b\nChronic stage - \nCardiomyopathy, \nMegacolon, \nMegaesophagus \nphase \n\u25cb\u200b\nCNS involvement \n\u25cb\u200b\nKerandel\u2019s Sign \n\u25a0\u200b\nDelayed \nsensation to \npain \n\u25cb\u200b\nSomnolence \n\u25a0\u200b\nGambian \nSleeping \nSickness \n\u25cf\u200b\nAntigenic Variation - turning \non or off the genes coding for \ntheir \nVariant \nSurface \nGlycoproteins, evade immune \nresponse  \nDiagnosis \n\u25cf\u200b\nComplete \nhistory \nfor \npossible exposure  \n\u25cf\u200b\nThick and thin blood smear  \n\u25cb\u200b\nView parasite in the \nblood (trypomastigote \nstage)  \n\u25cf\u200b\nMicrohematocrit  \n\u25cf\u200b\nSerology \n\u25cf\u200b\nXenodiagnosis  \n\u25cf\u200b\nBlood \nculture: \nNovy, \nMcNeal, & Nicolle (NNN)  \n\u25cf\u200b\nPCR  \n\u25cf\u200b\nExcision \nbiopsy \nof \nchagomas  \n\u25cf\u200b\n\u2605 AMASTIGOTES - inside \nthe cells or tissues \nTRYPOMASTIGOTES - \nblood \n\u25cf\u200b\nIdentification of parasite in body \nfluid or tissue \n\u25cf\u200b\nBuffy coat concentration if there \nis low parasitemia \n\u25cf\u200b\nSerologic tests \n\u25cf\u200b\nWHO \ncriteria \nfor \nCNS \ninvolvement: \n\u25cb\u200b\nIf there is increased protein in \nCSF (usually an increased \nIgM) \n= \nindicative \nfor \nthe \nmeningoencephalic stage  \n\u25cb\u200b\nIncreased CSF WBC count \n(>5/\u03bcL) \n\u25cf\u200b\nCard \nAgglutination \nTrypanosomiasis Test (CATT) \n \n\u25cf\u200b\nIdentification of parasite in body fluid \n\u25cb\u200b\nBlood: PBS, thick and thin smear \n\u25cb\u200b\nLymph \nnode \nfluid \nor \nCSF \nvia \naspiration or chancre biopsy \n\u25cf\u200b\nBuffy coat concentration (Microhematocrit \nconcentration) \n\u25cb\u200b\nCan \nbe \nused \nif \nthere \nis \nlow \nparasitemia \n\u25cf\u200b\nOther tests (serological): ELISA, IF, IHA \ntest, PCR \n\u25cf\u200b\nWHO Criteria for CNS Involvement: \n\u25cb\u200b\nIncreased protein (IgM) in CSF - \npathognomonic \nfor \nthe \nmeningoencephalic \nstage \nof \nthe \ndisease \n\u25cb\u200b\nWhite cell count of more than 5/uL \n \nTreatment \n\u25cf\u200b\nDOC: \nNifurtimox \nand \nBenznidazole  \n\u25cf\u200b\nAllopurinol and itraconazole  \n\u25cf\u200b\nSymptomatic and supportive \ntreatment  \n\u25cf\u200b\nSurgery - for the mega \nsyndromes  \n\u25cf\u200b\nPentamidine \n\u25cf\u200b\nOthers (available at CDC only): \n\u25cb\u200b\nSuramin \n\u25cb\u200b\nMelarsoprol \n\u25cb\u200b\nEflornithine \n\u25cb\u200b\nNifurtimox \n \n\u25cf\u200b\nPentamidine \n\u25cf\u200b\nOthers (available at CDC only) \n\u25cb\u200b\nSuramin, Melarsoprol, Eflornithine, \nand Nifurtimox \nPrevention \n\u25cf\u200b\nSpraying insecticide inside \nthe house \n\u25cf\u200b\nImprove \nhousing/living \nconditions \n\u25cf\u200b\nScreening \nof \nblood \ndonations \n\u25cf\u200b\nEarly \ndetection \nand \ntreatment of new cases \n\u25cf\u200b\nEarly diagnosis and early treatment \nof cases \n\u25cb\u200b\nPhysical examination \n\u25cf\u200b\nMinimizing contact with tsetse flies \n(Vector control) \n\u25cf\u200b\nEarly diagnosis and treatment of cases \n\u25cf\u200b\nMinimizing contact with tsetse flies and \nuse of insect repellant \n\u25cf\u200b\nWearing long-sleeved shirts and pants of \nmedium weight material in neutral colors \n\u25cf\u200b\nUse of PPEs to protect possible human \nhosts \n \n \n \nLet's capy-talize on our learning with these awesome notes!  |  13 \n"]}, {"title": "-PAR- 12 Plasmodia.docx.pdf", "pages": ["PARASITOLOGY \nPlasmodia \nA. Prof. Lourdes T. M. Dominguez, MD, MPH, FPSP | November 2025 | A.Y. 2025-2026 \n \n \nTABLE OF CONTENTS \n \n1. INTRODUCTION.......................................................................1 \n1.1. EPIDEMIOLOGY............................................................ 1 \n1.2. ETIOLOGY......................................................................2 \n2. MODE OF TRANSMISSION.....................................................2 \n3. MORPHOLOGY........................................................................2 \n3.1. STAGES OF PLASMODIA..............................................2 \n3.2. P. FALCIPARUM............................................................. 2 \n3.2.1. TROPHOZOITE (RING FORM)............................2 \n3.2.2. GAMETOCYTE.....................................................2 \n3.2.3. SCHIZONT........................................................... 2 \n3.3. P. VIVAX..........................................................................3 \n3.3.1. TROPHOZOITE (RING FORM)............................3 \n3.3.2. GAMETOCYTE.....................................................3 \n3.3.3. SCHIZONT........................................................... 3 \n3.4. P. MALARIAE..................................................................3 \n3.4.1. TROPHOZOITE (RING FORM)............................3 \n3.4.2. GAMETOCYTE.....................................................3 \n3.4.3. SCHIZONT........................................................... 3 \n3.5. P. OVALE........................................................................ 3 \n3.5.1. TROPHOZOITE (RING FORM)............................3 \n3.5.2. GAMETOCYTE.....................................................3 \n3.5.3. SCHIZONT........................................................... 3 \n4. LIFE CYCLE OF MALARIAL PARASITE................................ 4 \n4.1 EXO-ERYTHROCYTIC CYCLE (LIVER).........................4 \n4.2. ERYTHROCYTIC CYCLE (RBC)....................................4 \n4.3. SPOROGONIC CYCLE (MOSQUITO)........................... 4 \n4.4. RELAPSE....................................................................... 5 \n4.5. RECRUDESCENCE....................................................... 5 \n5. PATHOPHYSIOLOGY.............................................................. 5 \n5.1. TISSUE ANOXIA............................................................ 5 \n5.1.1 ANEMIA.................................................................5 \n5.1.1.1 INTRAVASCULAR.............................................. 5 \n5.1.1.2 EXTRAVASCULAR.............................................5 \n5.1.1.3 BONE MARROW DYSFUNCTION \n(DYSERYTHROPOIESIS)..............................................5 \n5.1.2 CHANGES IN BLOOD FLOW............................... 6 \n5.1.3 CHANGES IN CAPILLARY ENDOTHELIUM........ 6 \n5.1.4 HISTOTOXIC ANOXIA.......................................... 6 \n5.2. PARASITE VIRULENCE FACTORS...............................6 \n5.2.1 MULTIPLICATION CAPACITY...............................6 \n5.2.2 RED BLOOD CELL SELECTIVITY........................6 \n5.2.3 CYTOADHERENCE AND ROSETTING ABILITY. 6 \n5.2.4 POTENTIAL TO INDUCE CYTOKINE RELEASE. 6 \n5.2.5 ANTIGENICITY......................................................6 \n5.2.5 ANTIMALARIAL DRUG RESISTANCE................. 6 \n5.3 CLINICAL FEATURES.....................................................7 \n5.3.1  STAGES OF CLASSICAL MALARIAL \nPAROXYSMS................................................................. 7 \n5.3.2 ANEMIA.................................................................7 \n5.3.3. BLACK WATER FEVER....................................... 7 \n5.3.4. RENAL FAILURE..................................................7 \n5.3.5. LIVER DYSFUNCTION........................................ 7 \n5.3.6. ABNORMAL BLEEDING: COAGULOPATHY &....7 \nTHROMBOCYTOPENIA.................................................7 \n5.3.7. METABOLIC ABNORMALITIES...........................8 \n5.3.8 GI DYSFUNCTION................................................8 \n5.3.9. BACTERIAL SUPER OR CO INFECTIONS IN \nSEVERE MALARIA........................................................ 8 \n5.3.10. PULMONARY EDEMA....................................... 8 \n5.3.11. PLACENTAL DYSFUNCTION............................ 8 \n5.3.12. ALGID MALARIA................................................ 8 \n5.3.13. CEREBRAL MALARIA LEADING TO COMA.....8 \n6. LABORATORY DIAGNOSIS....................................................8 \n6.1. THICK AND THIN BLOOD SMEAR................................8 \n6.2. QUANTITATIVE BUFFY COAT.......................................9 \n6.3. MALARIA RAPID DIAGNOSTIC TESTS........................ 9 \n6.4. OTHER DIAGNOSTIC TESTS..................................... 10 \n7. TREATMENT.......................................................................... 10 \n8. PREVENTION & CONTROL.................................................. 10 \nX. INHERENT HOST FACTORS THAT OFFERS PROTECTION \nAGAINST MALARIAL INFECTION............................................10 \n9. PLASMODIUM KNOWLESI................................................... 10 \n9.1. EPIDEMIOLOGY.......................................................... 10 \n9.2. LIFE CYCLE................................................................. 10 \n9.3. RISK FACTORS............................................................10 \n9.4. PROTECTIVE FACTORS.............................................10 \n9.5. CLINICAL PRESENTATION......................................... 10 \n9.6. LABORATORY DIAGNOSIS.........................................10 \n9.6. METHODS OF DETECTION........................................ 11 \n9.7. MORPHOLOGY............................................................11 \n9.8 TREATMENT................................................................. 12 \n9.9 PREVENTION AND CONTROL.................................... 12 \n10. Q&A...................................................................................... 12 \nTRANS CORRECTION FORM...................................................12 \nSUMMARY TABLE.....................................................................13 \n \n \nLEGEND \n\ud83d\udcd7\u200b Reference Book \n\ud83d\udcd6\u200b Previous Trans \n\u2605\u200b Take Note / Important Info \n \n1. INTRODUCTION \n \n1.1. EPIDEMIOLOGY \n\u25cf\u200b\nWorldwide \n\u25cb\u200b\nMore common in tropics and subtropical areas like Africa \nand Southeast Asia \n\u25cb\u200b\nThere have been reports that malaria is seen in South \nAmerica. \n \nLet's make learning un-bear-ably fun \ud83d\udc3c  |  1 \n", "PARASITOLOGY | PLASMODIA \n\u25cf\u200b\n1 of 3 major infectious disease threats (Malaria, HIV/AIDS,TB) \n\u25cf\u200b\nPhilippines \n\u25cb\u200b\nEndemic in 49 of 81 provinces \n\u25a0\u200b\n73% Luzon, 4% Visayas, 23% Mindanao \n\u25cb\u200b\nHighest malaria cases were reported in Cagayan, \nIsabela, Palawan, Sulu, Tawi-Tawi \n\u25cb\u200b\nPeak transmission occurs at the beginning and end of \nrainy season \n\u2605 Principal Vector: Anopheles minimus var flavirostris, \nFEMALE \n\u25cb\u200b\nNight-biter \n\u25cb\u200b\nBreeds in slow-flowing, shaded streams \n \n1.2. ETIOLOGY \n \nPlasmodium falciparum \nMalignant tertian malaria \nPlasmodium vivax \nBenign tertian malaria \nPlasmodium malariae \nQuartan malarie \nPlasmodium ovale \nBenign tertian malaria \n \n\u25cf\u200b\nIn the Philippines, in order of frequency: \n\u25cb\u200b\nPlasmodium falciparum (Most frequent) \n\u25cb\u200b\nPlasmodium vivax \n\u25cb\u200b\nMixed infection (P. falciparum and P. vivax) \n\u25cb\u200b\nPlasmodium malariae \n\u25cb\u200b\nPlasmodium ovale \n\u25a0\u200b\nRare in the Philippines \n\u25a0\u200b\nMore common in Africa \n\u25cf\u200b\nVectors of Malaria in the Philippines \n\u25cb\u200b\nPrincipal Vector: Anopheles minimus var flavirostris, \nFEMALE \n\u25a0\u200b\nFemale bites because they need blood for formation \nof eggs, males feed on nectars of plants, etc. \n\u25cb\u200b\nSecondary Vectors: \n\u25a0\u200b\nAnopheles litoralis \n\u25a0\u200b\nAnopheles balabacensis \n\u25a0\u200b\nAnopheles mangyanus \n\u25a0\u200b\nAnopheles maculatus \n\u25cf\u200b\nMalaria is NOT a new disease and has been recorded since \nthe time of the pharaohs \n \n2. MODE OF TRANSMISSION \n\u25cf\u200b\nBite of the female anopheles mosquito (Most common) \n\u25cb\u200b\nFemale mosquitoes need the blood for the formation of \neggs \n\u25cf\u200b\nDirectly from one person to another by passage of blood \ncontaining erythrocytic parasites through: \n\u25cb\u200b\nBlood transfusion \n\u25cb\u200b\nSharing of contaminated syringes and needles \n\u25cb\u200b\nMingling of infected maternal blood with that of the infant \nduring the birth process \n\u25a0\u200b\nResults in neonatal malaria \n\u25cb\u200b\nTransplacental transmission \n\u25a0\u200b\nResults in congenital malaria \n \nPLASMODIUM SPP. GENERALITIES \nFinal Host \nMosquitoes (female Anopheles) \nIntermediate Host \nMan \nLocation in Humans \n(Habitat) \nLiver and RBCs \nInfective Stage to \nmosquito (FH) \nGametocytes \nInfective Stage to \nman (IH) \nSporozoites \nMode of \nTransmission \nMosquito bite, blood transfusion, \nneonatal/congenital \n\u2605 Mosquitoes are the FINAL HOST because the sexual cycle \noccurs inside them. Man is only the INTERMEDIATE HOST. \n \n3. MORPHOLOGY \n \n3.1. STAGES OF PLASMODIA \n\u25cf\u200b\nAsexual stage: happens in intermediate host (man) \n\u25cb\u200b\nSchizont \n\u25cb\u200b\nTrophozoite \n\u25cf\u200b\nSexual stage: happens in final host (mosquito) \n\u25cb\u200b\nMacrogametocyte: Female  \n\u25a0\u200b\nNucleus is more dense \n\u25cb\u200b\nMicrogametocyte: Male \n\u25a0\u200b\nPale nucleus, forms loose network \n \n3.2. P. FALCIPARUM \n\u25cf\u200b\nMalignant tertian malaria  \n\u25cf\u200b\nMost pathogenic of all plasmodium organisms \n\u25cf\u200b\nResponsible for most deaths caused by malaria \n\u25cf\u200b\nTropics and subtropics \n\u25cf\u200b\nRBCs are NOT enlarged; normal  \n\u25cf\u200b\nStippling present (Maurer\u2019s dots) \n\u25cb\u200b\nFalciparum: Maurer - Ferdinand Marcos \n\u25cf\u200b\nRing forms and gametocytes seen on PBS, other stages \n(schizonts) are rarely seen unless patient is heavily infected \n \n3.2.1. TROPHOZOITE (RING FORM) \n\u25cf\u200b\nVery small, \u2159 of the size of the RBC \n\u25cf\u200b\nWith 1 or 2 nuclei \n\u25cf\u200b\n>1 ring form can infect 1 RBC (multiple infection) \n\u25cf\u200b\nApplique or accol\u00e9 form (looks like a headphone) usually \nfound on the periphery of the cell \n\u2605 The first thing to check when looking through slides for malarial \ninfection would be the size of the infected RBCs and compare \nthem to those that are not infected. \n \n3.2.2. GAMETOCYTE \n\u25cf\u200b\n\u2605 Crescent or banana-shaped \n \n3.2.3. SCHIZONT \n\u25cf\u200b\nUsually not seen in the PB \n\u25cf\u200b\n8-24 merozoites \n\u25cf\u200b\nUsually sequestered in the internal capillaries \n \n \nFig 3.2.3-1. P. falciparum morphology \n(Source: Lecture video) \nTrophozoite: Multiple infections (1-3) in one red cell. Red cells are normal \nsized. \nGametocyte: crescent/banana-shaped \nSchizont: Merozoites inside the schizonts. Each merozoite can infect \nanother cell \n \n \nLet's make learning un-bear-ably fun \ud83d\udc3c  |  2 \n", "PARASITOLOGY | PLASMODIA \n3.3. P. VIVAX \n\u25cf\u200b\nBenign tertian malaria  \n\u25cf\u200b\n2nd most common species in the Philippines \n\u25cf\u200b\nPredominant malarial parasite in most parts of the world  \n\u25cf\u200b\nRBCs are enlarged  \n\u25cb\u200b\nVivax = Vig RBC \n\u25cf\u200b\nStippling present (Schuffner\u2019s dots) \n\u25cb\u200b\nVivax: Schuffner\u2019s - Vilma Santos \n\u25cf\u200b\nAll stages seen on PBS \n \n3.3.1. TROPHOZOITE (RING FORM) \n\u25cf\u200b\nOccupies \u2153 of the size of the RBC; 1 infection per RBC \n\u25cf\u200b\nInfected RBCs are enlarged \n\u25cf\u200b\nPigmented but not that much compared to P. malariae \n\u25cf\u200b\nRed dot with blue  \n \n3.3.2. GAMETOCYTE \n\u25cf\u200b\nRound or ovoid \n\u25cf\u200b\nLarge, may almost fill the RBC \n\u25cf\u200b\nPigmented \n\u25cf\u200b\nMicro and macrogametocytes \n \n3.3.3. SCHIZONT \n\u25cf\u200b\nAmeboid shaped  \n\u25cf\u200b\nLarge may almost fill the RBC \n\u25cf\u200b\nPigmented \n\u25cf\u200b\n12-24 merozoites per schizont \n \n \nFig 3.3.3-1. P. vivax morphology \n(Source: Lecture video) \nTrophozoite: Prominent red dot with a dark staining cytoplasmic ring. Red \ncells are enlarged \nGametocyte: Large and may fill up the whole cell. Brownish pigment can \nbe appreciated \nSchizont: Brownish pigment can be appreciated \n \nBrown pigment seen in the PBS is either P. vivax or P. malariae \n \n3.4. P. MALARIAE \n\u25cf\u200b\nQuartan malaria \n\u25cf\u200b\nOccurs primarily in subtropical and temperate areas \n\u25cf\u200b\nRBCs are NOT enlarged; normal \n\u25cf\u200b\nStippling present (Ziemann\u2019s dots) \n\u25cb\u200b\nMalariae: Ziemann\u2019s - Manila Zoo \n\u25cf\u200b\nAll stages seen on PBS  \n \n3.4.1. TROPHOZOITE (RING FORM) \n\u25cf\u200b\nOccupies \u2153 of the size of the RBC; 1 infection per RBC \n\u25cf\u200b\nResembles P. vivax but cytoplasm is thicker and heavily \npigmented \n\u25cf\u200b\nStretched across the diameter of the RBC called the band \nform  \n\u25cf\u200b\nHeavily pigmented \n \n3.4.2. GAMETOCYTE \n\u25cf\u200b\nSame as P. vivax except smaller \n\u25cf\u200b\nPigmented \n \n3.4.3. SCHIZONT \n\u25cf\u200b\n6-12 \nmerozoites \nforming \nrosette \n(rounded/flower-like \nstructure) \n\u25cf\u200b\nAlmost fills the cell \n\u25cf\u200b\nPigmented \n \n \nFig 3.4.3-1. P. malariae morphology \n(Source: Lecture video) \nTrophozoite: Less darker-stained and less prominent than  P. vivax. Band \nform is pigmented \nGametocyte: Prominent pigments \nSchizont: Merozoites form a rosette formation \n \n3.5. P. OVALE \n\u25cf\u200b\nTertian malaria \n\u25cf\u200b\nNot present in the Philippines but common in Africa \n\u25cf\u200b\nOccasionally reported in South America and Asia \n\u25cf\u200b\nRBCs are enlarged; oval or elongated in shape with ragged \nedges \n\u25cf\u200b\nStippling present (Schuffner\u2019s/James\u2019 dots) \n\u25cb\u200b\nOvale: James\u2019 - Orange Juice \n\u25cf\u200b\nAll stages seen on PBS \n \n3.5.1. TROPHOZOITE (RING FORM) \n\u25cf\u200b\nResembles P. vivax but more compact \n\u25cf\u200b\nMultiple infection in 1 RBC may be seen like P. falciparum \n \n3.5.2. GAMETOCYTE \n\u25cf\u200b\nMale and female gametocyte are large, nearly occupies entire \ncell \n \n3.5.3. SCHIZONT \n\u25cf\u200b\nResembles P. malariae but pigment is darker \n\u25cf\u200b\n8 merozoites arranged in a central block of pigment usually \nseen  \n \n \nLet's make learning un-bear-ably fun \ud83d\udc3c  |  3 \n", "PARASITOLOGY | PLASMODIA \n \nFig 3.5.3-1. P. ovale morphology \n(Source: Lecture video) \nTrophozoite: Multiple infections in one red cell. Red cells are enlarged \nGametocyte: Also large but they may occupy or fill up the whole cell  \nSchizont: Merozoites inside the schizonts. They are arranged as such with \na pigment at the center \n \nMNEMONICS FOR STIPPLINGS \n\u25cf\u200b\nFerdinand Marcos = falciparum Maurer\u2019s dots \n\u25cf\u200b\nVilma Santos = vivax Schuffner\u2019s dots \n\u25cf\u200b\nManila Zoo = malariae Ziemman\u2019s dots \n\u25cf\u200b\nOrange Juice - ovale James\u2019 dots \n \n4. LIFE CYCLE OF MALARIAL PARASITE \n \n \n4.1 EXO-ERYTHROCYTIC CYCLE (LIVER) \n\u25cf\u200b\nThe sporozoites will enter the circulation and infect the \nhepatocytes. \n\u25cf\u200b\nInside the infected liver cells, sporozoites will divide several \ntimes and mature to become schizonts. \n\u25cb\u200b\nInside the schizonts will be the merozoites. \n\u2750 Merozoites are produced from asexual replication in the \nliver. \n\u25cf\u200b\nEach schizont in the hepatocyte will later on rupture and \nrelease the merozoites in the bloodstream. \n\u25cf\u200b\nInfective stage: Sporozoites \n\u25cb\u200b\nLocated in the proboscis of mosquito \n\u25cb\u200b\nInjected during a mosquito bite \n\u25cb\u200b\nOnce in the patient, it goes directly to the liver \n(exo-erythrocytic cycle) developing into merozoites that \nare contained in the schizont. \n\u25cb\u200b\nMerozoites will then infect the red cells, then transform \ninto trophozoites (some become gametocytes) \n\u25cf\u200b\nGametocytes, once ingested by a mosquito, will again \ncontinue the cycle inside the mosquito (Sporogonic cycle) \n \n\u2605 SUMMARY \nFemale mosquito bites the potential human host \u2192 injects the \nsporozoites \u2192 sporozoites inside the body infect the liver cells \n\u2192 sporozoites continually divide and form schizonts \u2192 inside the \nschizonts are merozoites \u2192 hepatocytes and schizonts rupture \u2192 \nmerozoites are released into the circulation \n \n\u2605 In case of P . vivax and P . ovale: \n\u25cf\u200b\nSchizonts can enter a dormant stage (Hypnozoites) \nand they can persist in the liver \n\u25cf\u200b\nThese can cause relapse in infection weeks or years \nlater \n\u25cf\u200b\nPatient becomes asymptomatic and then weeks/years \nlater, hypnozoites would become active and will be \nreleased or go back into the cycle and that would give \nrise again to the manifestation of malaria in the patient \n \n4.2. ERYTHROCYTIC CYCLE (RBC) \n\u25cf\u200b\nAfter the exo-erythrocytic cycle, the parasites undergo \nasexual multiplication in the erythrocytes. \n\u25cb\u200b\nAsexual phase or Erythrocytic cycle (\u2750 \nErythrocytic schizogony) \n\u25cf\u200b\nThese merozoites released from the ruptured schizonts \ninfect the red cells in circulation and develop into the ring \nstage trophozoites. \n\u25cf\u200b\nThen they would mature into erythrocytic schizonts \nwhich contain the differentiating or developing \nmerozoites. \n\u25cf\u200b\nSchizonts would rupture and release merozoites that can \ninfect other RBC. \n \n\u2750 If a large number of parasitic metabolites is released in \nthe bloodstream, it causes malarial paroxysms \n \n\u2605 Asexual cycle is synchronous, periodic, and \nspecies-specific \n\u2605 This cycle seems similar to the exo-erythrocytic cycle. Thus, \nremember: \n\u25cf\u200b\nExo-erythrocytic cycle would involve the hepatic cells. \n\u25cf\u200b\nErythrocytic cycle would involve the red cells. \n \n4.3. SPOROGONIC CYCLE (MOSQUITO) \n\u25cf\u200b\nThe parasite\u2019s sexual stage must occur in the body of the \nmosquito. \n\u25cf\u200b\nThe parasite\u2019s multiplication in the mosquito is known as the \nSporogonic Cycle \n\u25cf\u200b\nInside the mosquito\u2019s gut, the microgametes will meet the \nmacrogametes \n\u25cb\u200b\nAs the blood temperature falls, microgametocyte (male) \nmatures into microgametes via extrusion (exflagellation); \nmacrogametocyte matures into macrogamete. \n\u25cf\u200b\nFertilization occurs, generating the zygote. \n\u25cf\u200b\nThe zygote will differentiate to become the motile and \nelongated ookinete. \n\u25cf\u200b\nThis ookinete will invade the midgut wall of the mosquito \nwhere they will develop as oocyst. \n\u25cf\u200b\nThe oocyst will grow, rupture, and release sporozoites \n \n \nLet's make learning un-bear-ably fun \ud83d\udc3c  |  4 \n", "PARASITOLOGY | PLASMODIA \n\u25cb\u200b\nHaploid sporozoites \n\u25cb\u200b\nThe cycle may vary from 8 days (P. vivax) to 35 days (P. \nmalariae). \n\u25cb\u200b\nThen the sporozoites will migrate to the mosquito\u2019s \nsalivary glands. \n\u25cf\u200b\nWhen the infected mosquito bites a human host, inoculation of \nthe sporozoites into the human allows the continuation of the \nmalarial life cycle \n \n\u2605 SUMMARY \nFemale Anopheles mosquito bites an infected host \u2192 picks up \ngametocyte (microgametocytes and macrogametocytes meet) \u2192 \nSporogonic Cycle (Sexual Phase) \u2192 forms ookinete \u2192 ookinete \ninvade the midgut wall \u2192 forms oocyst \u2192 rupture and release \nsporozoites \u2192 migrate into mosquito\u2019s salivary glands (ready to be \nreleased when mosquito bites another human host) \n \n4.4. RELAPSE \n\u25cf\u200b\nHappens for P . vivax and P . ovale \n\u25cf\u200b\nSome of the schizonts containing sporozoites do not develop \nfurther and remain dormant in the liver as hypnozoites \n\u25cf\u200b\nWeeks or years later on, if there is reactivation of \nhypnozoites: \n\u25cb\u200b\nHypnozoites can enter the erythrocytic cycle (from \nhypnozoites, they will be reactivated and will release \nmerozoites into the cycle and the merozoites could infect \nthe erythrocytes) \u2192 relapse of malaria \n \n4.5. RECRUDESCENCE \n\u25cf\u200b\nHappens for: P . falciparum and P . malariae \n\u25cf\u200b\nDue to the persistence of drug-resistant parasites after \ncompletion of therapy \n\u25cf\u200b\nLong-term survival of erythrocytic stages at persistent \nundetectable levels of parasites in blood. \n\u25cb\u200b\nBut, if we do blood tests, the blood levels of the parasite \nare undetectable or very low \n\u25cb\u200b\nAfter some time, patients will again show signs and \nsymptoms \n \n5. PATHOPHYSIOLOGY \n \nFig 5-1. Pathophysiology of Malaria \n \n5.1. TISSUE ANOXIA \n\u25cf\u200b\nBasic pathophysiology of the changes seen in malaria is \nbrought about by TISSUE ANOXIA \n\u25cf\u200b\n4 Mechanisms leading to tissue anoxia: \n\u25cb\u200b\nAnemia: Because of the hemolysis that is occurring \n\u25a0\u200b\nRemember in the erythrocytic cycle, RBCs rupture to \nrelease merozoites \n\u25cb\u200b\nChanges in blood flow \n\u25cb\u200b\nChanges in capillary endothelium \n\u25cb\u200b\nHistotoxic anoxia \n\u25cf\u200b\nAll of these are brought about by the mediators that are \nstimulated to be released because of the organism or toxic \nmetabolites produced by the parasite. \n \n5.1.1 ANEMIA \n\u25cf\u200b\nDue to hemolysis which may be classified as intravascular or \nextravascular \n \n5.1.1.1 INTRAVASCULAR \n\u25cf\u200b\nDue to the rupture of infected and non-infected RBC \n\u25cb\u200b\nRecall: When the hepatic schizonts rupture, they release \nthe hepatic merozoites into the bloodstream and these \nwill invade the RBCs in the circulation \u2192 life cycle will \ncontinue in the RBC (the parasite enters the erythrocytic \ncycle wherein they will develop to be the trophozoite and \nlater becomes the merozoite). \n\u25cf\u200b\nWhen the RBCs rupture, the parasites are released, and this \nhemolysis due to the mechanical rupture may lead to anemia \n(RBCs are destroyed) \n \n5.1.1.2 EXTRAVASCULAR \n\u25cf\u200b\nPhagocytosis \nof \ninfected \nand \nnon-infected \nRBC \nby \nreticuloendothelial cells. \n\u25cb\u200b\nReducing number of RBC in circulation resulting in \nanemia \n\u25cf\u200b\nINFECTED RBCs: \n\u25cb\u200b\nWhen merozoites invade the RBC, the RBCs will have: \n\u25a0\u200b\nReduced deformability due to cytoskeletal changes \n\u25a0\u200b\nIncreased membrane stiffness \n\u25a0\u200b\nIncreased cytoplasm viscosity \n\u25cb\u200b\nFiltered by the spleen reticuloendothelial cells because of \nthe extravascular changes. \n\u25cf\u200b\nNON-INFECTED RBCs \n\u25cb\u200b\nAfter the release of merozoites from infected RBC, they \ncome into contact with non-infected RBCs and release \ntheir contents. \n\u25cb\u200b\nEnzymes: proteases, phospholipases, lipids \n\u25a0\u200b\nCan induce changes in the skeletal components of \nRBC membrane \n\u25cb\u200b\nParasite protein released in the plasma may be deposited \nin the membrane of the non-infected RBCs \n\u25a0\u200b\nCan alter the antigenic and vascular properties of the \nnon-infected RBC \u2192 removal \n\u25cf\u200b\nENDPOINT: Whether infected or non-infected RBCs \n\u25cb\u200b\nAltered properties \n\u25a0\u200b\nFiltered in the spleen by reticuloendothelial cells \n\u25a0\u200b\nIncreased destruction and removal of infected and \nnon-infected RBCs \n\u25a0\u200b\nEventually leads to ANEMIA \n \n5.1.1.3 \nBONE \nMARROW \nDYSFUNCTION \n(DYSERYTHROPOIESIS) \n\u25cf\u200b\nPersist for days or weeks following acute malaria \n\u25cf\u200b\nNOT due to deficiency of erythropoietin production, but due to \nthe involvement of cytokines and other mediators of \ninflammation \n\u25cf\u200b\nCytokines play an important role in the maturation of erythroid \ncells \n\u25cb\u200b\nIL-6: Induction of hepcidin expression \n\u25a0\u200b\nHepcidin: Master regulator of iron \n\u25cb\u200b\nIn the presence of inflammation, hepcidin secretion is \n \n \nLet's make learning un-bear-ably fun \ud83d\udc3c  |  5 \n", "PARASITOLOGY | PLASMODIA \nhigh due to the increased release of inflammation \nmediators \n\u25a0\u200b\nSince Hepcidin is the master regulator of iron, the \navailability of iron is affected \n\u25cb\u200b\nWith Hepcidin, Iron is trapped in macrophages and \nhepatocytes \n\u25a0\u200b\nDecreased absorption of iron in the intestine OR \n\u25a0\u200b\nDecline in iron availability needed for erythropoiesis \n\u2192 ANEMIA \n \n\u2605 With malaria \u2192 inflammation occurs \u2192 release of cytokines \n\u2192 affecting secretion of hepcidin \u2192 high secretion of hepcidin \n\u2192 iron availability is affected \u2192 iron gets trapped in \nmacrophages & hepatocytes \u2192 decline in availability of iron \nneeded for erythropoiesis \u2192 ANEMIA \n \n5.1.2 CHANGES IN BLOOD FLOW \n\u25cf\u200b\nBlockage of small blood vessels \n\u25cb\u200b\nCytoadherence: Mature forms of parasite (P. falciparum) \nfirst roll on and the adhere to the microvascular \nepithelium \n\u25a0\u200b\nFormation of \u201cknobs\u201d that contain rosettins, repeated \ninterspersed families (RIFINs), histidine-rich proteins \n(HRP), \nPlasmodium \nfalciparum \nerythrocyte \nmembrane protein 1 (PfEMP-1) \n\u25cb\u200b\nPfEMP-1: Most adhesive among the knobs \n\u25cb\u200b\nRosettin \nand \nPfEMP-1: \nResponsible \nfor \nrosette \nformation of cells \n\u25cb\u200b\nReceptors of Rosettin and PfEMP-1 are found on the \nparasitized and non-parasitized cells, as well as platelets, \nbrain tissue, and placental tissue \n\u25cf\u200b\nDue to the proteins, parasitized cells would adhere to one \nanother and to the microvascular epithelium \u2192 vessels are \nclogged by parasitized cells leading to tissue ischemia \n\u25cb\u200b\nReduction in deformability of the parasitized erythrocytes \n\u25cb\u200b\nConsequences \nof \nmicrocirculatory \nobstruction \n(cytoadherence & clogging of blood vessels) \n\u25a0\u200b\nReduced oxygen and substrate supply leading to \nanaerobic glycolysis and lactic acidosis \n\u25cf\u200b\nVasomotor changes: Vasoconstriction of arterioles and \nvenules, vasodilation of capillaries \n\u25cb\u200b\nTissue would sense they lack blood flow \n\u25a0\u200b\nVasoconstriction increases blood flow to important \norgans \n\u25a0\u200b\nVasodilation promotes better entry of blood carrying \noxygen to the tissues \n \n5.1.3 CHANGES IN CAPILLARY ENDOTHELIUM \n\u25cf\u200b\nIncreased permeability \u2192 cerebral edema, hypovolemic shock \nand congestion \n\u25cb\u200b\nLeads to tissue infarction and necrosis because of fluid \nleak \n \n5.1.4 HISTOTOXIC ANOXIA \n\u25cf\u200b\nImpaired cellular respiration due to oxidative phosphorylation \nof the mitochondria is inhibited \n \n5.2. PARASITE VIRULENCE FACTORS \n \n5.2.1 MULTIPLICATION CAPACITY \n\u25cf\u200b\nA rapidly expanding biomass of parasites is more likely to \noutstrip host defenses \n\u25cb\u200b\nInitially, there would be an increase in the parasite \ndivision \u2192 more parasites are being produced during \nearly phase of infection \u2192 outnumbers host defense \n\u25cb\u200b\nEventually immunity will be mounted by the host and \ndevelops immunity \n\u25cf\u200b\nImmunity reduces multiplication and then limits the size of \nparasite biomass \n \n5.2.2 RED BLOOD CELL SELECTIVITY \n\u25cf\u200b\nUnselective parasite will not be limited by the availability of the \nsuitable red cells \n\u25cb\u200b\nP. vivax prefers young RBC \n\u25a0\u200b\nP. vivax \u2192 young RBC \u2192 \u201cVATA\u201d \n\u25cb\u200b\nP. malariae prefers old RBC \n\u25a0\u200b\nP. malariae \u2192 old RBC \u2192 \u201cMATANDA\u201d \n\u25cb\u200b\nP. falciparum infects ALL stages of RBC \u2192 MOST \nPROBLEMATIC (any age of the RBCs) \n\u25cf\u200b\nBecause of this selectivity, this also affects the biomass of the \nparasite \n\u25cb\u200b\nMore younger RBCs \u2192 more P. vivax can infect \n\u25cb\u200b\nMore older RBCs \u2192 more P. malariae can infect \n \n5.2.3 CYTOADHERENCE AND ROSETTING ABILITY \n\u25cf\u200b\nParasites with both of these adhesive characteristics may be \nmore pathogenic \n\u25cb\u200b\nCan plug circulation and cause anoxia \n\u25cf\u200b\nAll four Plasmodium spp. normally infecting man can \ninduce rosetting but: \n\u25cb\u200b\nONLY P. falciparum causes lethal infection \n\u25cb\u200b\nONLY P. falciparum causes cytoadherence and all \nparasites sequester in vivo - MOST PROBLEMATIC \n \n \n \n5.2.4 POTENTIAL TO INDUCE CYTOKINE RELEASE \n\u25cf\u200b\nCytokines are responsible for many of the symptoms and \nsigns of malaria, particularly fever and malaise. \n\u25cf\u200b\nAside from these, it can also cause anemia. \n \n5.2.5 ANTIGENICITY \n\u25cf\u200b\nParasites that are not recognized by the host will obviously \nhave a relative growth advantage. \n\u25cf\u200b\nIf the host cannot mount an immune response (as it doesn\u2019t \nrecognize the parasite), then the more parasites would be \nable to escape immunity and they would flourish in the host \nbody. \n5.2.5 ANTIMALARIAL DRUG RESISTANCE \n\u25cf\u200b\nIn many areas of the tropics, antimalarial drugs are widely \navailable and self-medication is common. \n\u25cf\u200b\nIf the user will feel well, it could contribute to the formation of \n \n \nLet's make learning un-bear-ably fun \ud83d\udc3c  |  6 \n", "PARASITOLOGY | PLASMODIA \ndrug resistance \n\u27a2\u200b REMEMBER: When the cell ruptures, it releases schizonts \n(merozoites) and its contents \n \n \nOTHER EFFECTS OF CYTOKINES \n\u25cf\u200b\nPlacental dysfunction (problem in pregnant women) \n\u25cf\u200b\nSuppression of erythropoiesis (due to hepcidin \u2192 controls \nthe availability of iron that will be used for erythropoiesis) \n\u25cf\u200b\nHepatic dysfunction \n\u25cf\u200b\nInhibition of gluconeogenesis \n\u25cf\u200b\nPromote cytoadherence by up-regulating the endothelial \nexpression of some vascular ligand for P. falciparum infected \nRBC particularly ICAM-I (the principal receptor of cerebral \nvascular epithelium) \n\u25cb\u200b\nMediators of parasite killing by activating leukocytes and \nother cells to release nitric oxide generating parasiticidal \nlipid peroxides. \n \n5.3 CLINICAL FEATURES \n\u25cf Clinical presentations of malaria: \n\u25cb Headache \n\u25cb Fatigue \n\u25cb Vomiting \n\u25cb Chills \n\u25cb Fever \n\u25cb Muscle aches \n\u25cf Paroxysms of fever, weakness & exhaustion, aching bones, \nlimbs, back, loss of appetite, diarrhea, epigastric pain \n\u25cf 3 stages of classical malaria paroxysms: \n\u25cb Cold stage \n\u25cb Hot stage \n\u25cb Sweating stage \n \n5.3.1  STAGES OF CLASSICAL MALARIAL PAROXYSMS \n \nCold stage \nSudden onset of feeling cold, shivering, \nteeth chattering, shaking of entire body, high \nor rising temperature, intense peripheral \nvasoconstriction, febrile convulsions  \n \nRigor lasts 10-15 minutes (e.g. due to fever \n- that is why the patient feels cold). \n \nHot stage \nFlushed and hot, headache, tachypneic, \ndelirious lasting for 2-6 hours (P. vivax and \nP. ovale), 6 hours or more (P. malariae), \nlonger (P. falciparum). \nSweating stage \nDefervescence, diaphoresis, temperatures \nlowers down in 2-4 hours. \n \n\u25cf\u200b\nBlood Stage: responsible for the clinical manifestations of the \ninfection. \n\u25cf\u200b\nWhen the parasites reach the end of the erythrocytic cycle, \nthe parasitized red cells rupture and release: \n\u25cb\u200b\nMerozoites: can infect new RBCs \n\u25cb\u200b\nProducts of metabolism of the parasites and RBCs \n\u25cf\u200b\nThese toxic metabolites and cytokines are thought to be the \ncause behind the malarial paroxysms \n\u25cf\u200b\nDepending on the species the occurrence of the paroxysm \nmay occur as such. \n \n \n5.3.2 ANEMIA \n\u25cf\u200b\nDue to hemolysis which may be classified as intravascular or \nextravascular \n \n5.3.3. BLACK WATER FEVER \n\u25cf\u200b\nRefers to massive intravascular hemolysis and the \npassage of dark red, brown, or usually black urine \n\u25cf\u200b\nOccurs in P. falciparum but has been reported in vivax and \nquartan malaria (P. malariae) as well \n\u25cf\u200b\nCauses remain unknown: \n\u25cb\u200b\nCould be due to autoimmune response due to the \ndevelopment of antibodies to the parasitized red cells \n\u25cb\u200b\nCan develop after quinine therapy \n\u25a0\u200b\nBut even patients who did not undergo quinine \ntherapy acquired Black Water Fever \n \n5.3.4. RENAL FAILURE \n\u25cf\u200b\nCommon manifestation of P. falciparum in infected adults \n\u25cf\u200b\nBasic pathology is acute tubular necrosis resulting from \nred \ncell \nsludging \n(cytoadherence, \nrosette formation, \nparasitized red cell sequestration) \u2192 plug up the renal \ncirculation \u2192 decreased renal blood flow \u2192 renal tissue \nanoxia \n\u25cf\u200b\nSequestration is greatest in the medullary vessels and it is \nin the medulla where tissue PO2 is lowest and most \nvulnerable to ischemia and further hypoxia \n \n5.3.5. LIVER DYSFUNCTION \n\u25cf\u200b\nLiver is the first organ to be attached by the parasite \n\u25cf\u200b\nJaundice is common \n\u25cf\u200b\nDecreased clotting factor synthesis \n\u25cf\u200b\nDecreased metabolic clearance of drugs \n\u25cf\u200b\nDecreased biliary excretion \n\u25cf\u200b\nFailure of gluconeogenesis \u2192 Lactic acidosis and \nHypoglycemia \n \n5.3.6. ABNORMAL BLEEDING: COAGULOPATHY & \nTHROMBOCYTOPENIA \n\u25cf\u200b\nDerangement in blood coagulation and platelet function may \nbe due to accelerated coagulation cascade activity by: \n\u25cb\u200b\nAccelerated fibrinogen turnover \n\u25cb\u200b\nConsumption of antithrombin III, protein C, and protein S \n\u25cb\u200b\nIncreased \nconcentrations \nof \nplasminogen activator \n \n \nLet's make learning un-bear-ably fun \ud83d\udc3c  |  7 \n", "PARASITOLOGY | PLASMODIA \ninhibitor-I and fibrin degradation products \n\u25cb\u200b\nThrombocytopenia due to increased splenic clearance \n\u25cb\u200b\nDuring rosette formation, even platelets are affected \n\u25cb\u200b\nIf any cellular anomaly is detected \u2192 increased in splenic \nsequestration and clearance \n\u25cb\u200b\nLiver being affected, even the coagulation factors \nbecause these are where they are produced. \n\u25a0\u200b\nPatients become more prone to bleeding \n \n5.3.7. METABOLIC ABNORMALITIES \n\u25cf\u200b\nHypoglycemia \n\u25cb\u200b\nAssociated closely with hyperlactatemia (increased blood \nlactate levels) \n\u25cb\u200b\nAdults: due to increased glucose demand \n\u25cb\u200b\nChildren: due to reduced glucose supply; impaired \nhepatic gluconeogenesis \n\u25cb\u200b\nStimulation of pancreatic B cell insulin secretion in \nquinine treated patients (occurs after the first 24 hours of \ntreatment) \n\u25cf\u200b\nAcidosis \n\u25cb\u200b\nMainly caused by lactic acid due to: \n\u25a0\u200b\nTissue anaerobic glycolysis \n\u25a0\u200b\nReduced circulating red cells \n\u25a0\u200b\nReduced oxygen carriage \n\u25a0\u200b\nIncreased glycolysis (hypermetabolic state) \n\u25a0\u200b\nDecreased hepatic and renal lactate clearance \n\u25a0\u200b\nLactate production by malaria parasites \n \n5.3.8 GI DYSFUNCTION \n\u25cf\u200b\nMinor stress ulceration of the stomach and duodenum \n\u25cf\u200b\nMalabsorption due to reduced splanchnic perfusion \n\u25cb\u200b\nSecondary \nto \ngut \nsequestration \nand \nvisceral \nvasoconstriction \n \n5.3.9. BACTERIAL SUPER OR CO INFECTIONS IN SEVERE \nMALARIA \n\u25cf\u200b\nBroader immunosuppression due to: \n\u25cb\u200b\nDefects in monocyte and neutrophil chemotaxis \n\u25cb\u200b\nReduced monocytic phagocytic function \n \n5.3.10. PULMONARY EDEMA \n\u25cf\u200b\nResults from sudden increase in pulmonary capillary \npermeability that is not reflected in other vascular body \n\u25cf\u200b\nCause of increase in permeability is not known, but may be \ndue to: \n\u25cb\u200b\nOverzealous parenteral fluid administration \n\u25cb\u200b\nPatient is going into DIC or anoxia that could affect the \nmicrocirculation (pulmonary circulation) \n \n5.3.11. PLACENTAL DYSFUNCTION \n\u25cf\u200b\nPregnancy increases susceptibility to malaria due to \nsuppression of systemic and placental cell-mediated immune \nresponse \n\u25cf\u200b\nIntense sequestration of falciparum infected RBCs in the \nplacenta leading to thickened syncytiotrophoblast, abnormal \nuteroplacental blood flow, and placental insufficiency \n\u25cb\u200b\nResults in fetal growth retardation \n\u25cb\u200b\nPfEMP-1 receptors are also found in the placenta \n\u25a0\u200b\nPromote cytoadherence and rosetting \n\u25a0\u200b\nResulting in plugging of the microvasculature leading \nto decreased blood flow (tissue anoxia) \u2192 fetal \ngrowth retardation \n \n5.3.12. ALGID MALARIA \n\u25cf\u200b\nSeen in falciparum malaria attacks \n\u25cf\u200b\nRapid development of hypotension and impairment of \nvascular perfusion \n\u25cf\u200b\nRapid fail in temperature, delirium, symptoms of generalized \nvascular collapse, and shock \n\u25cf\u200b\nMay be due to Gram-negative septicemia since malaria \npatients are more prone to bacterial coinfections May also be \ndue to pulmonary edema, massive GI bleeding, splenic \nrupture, and uncorrected dehydration \n \n5.3.13. CEREBRAL MALARIA LEADING TO COMA \n\u25cf\u200b\nAssociated with 15-20% mortality \n\u25cf\u200b\nMost serious complication of falciparum malaria infection \n\u25cf\u200b\nAbout 90% of children and 98% of adult survivors recover \nwithout sequelae \n\u25cf\u200b\nInvolve neurotransmitter abnormalities and nitric oxide which \nis a potent neurotransmitter inhibitor \n\u25cb\u200b\nNitric oxide is a toxin that is released when schizonts \nrupture \n\u25cf\u200b\nComa may have a sudden onset \n\u25cf\u200b\nSome \npatients \nmay \ncomplain \nof severe headaches, \ndrowsiness, and confusion before going into a coma \n \nFig. 5.3.13-1. Pathophysiology of Malaria \n(Source: Lecture Video) \n \n6. LABORATORY DIAGNOSIS \n\u25cf\u200b\nThick and thin blood smears \n\u25cf\u200b\nQuantitative buffy coat \n\u25cf\u200b\nMalaria rapid diagnostic tests \n\u25cf\u200b\nSerologic test: \n\u25cb\u200b\nIndirect Hemagglutination Assay (IHA) \n\u25cb\u200b\nEnzyme Linked Immunosorbent Assay (ELISA) \n\u25cf\u200b\nMolecular \n\u25cb\u200b\nPCR \n \n6.1. THICK AND THIN BLOOD SMEAR \n\u25cf\u200b\nGold standard; still the mainstay for diagnosis \n\u25cb\u200b\nTo rule out the malarial infection/fever especially if the \npatient has recently visited endemic areas and to follow \nup therapy \n\u25cf\u200b\nGiemsa or Wright\u2019s stain \n\u25cf\u200b\nHow often? \n\u25cb\u200b\nNo standard frequency, but every 6-12 hours is usually \nappropriate \n\u25cf\u200b\nBlood sample preferably taken when? \n\u25cb\u200b\nDuring the febrile episode because this is the time the \nparasites are released when the cells are ruptured \n\u25cf\u200b\nUntil when? \n\u25cb\u200b\nNeed to follow up and do parasite counts to check that \nthe burden is going down \n\u25cb\u200b\nIt depends on the attending physicians. They usually do \nthis every day until there will be days in between. \n\u25cf\u200b\nThick smears are done first to screen for the parasite\u2019s \n \n \nLet's make learning un-bear-ably fun \ud83d\udc3c  |  8 \n", "PARASITOLOGY | PLASMODIA \npresence or absence. If it is present, a thin smear is done for \nspeciation. \n\u25cb\u200b\nExperienced \nmicroscopists can sometimes already \nidentify the species from the thick smear (ex. when P. \nfalciparum gametocyte is seen). \n \nTHIN SMEAR \nTHICK SMEAR \nLooks like the usual peripheral \nblood smear \nWater is added to lyse RBC to \nfacilitate the release of the \nparasites \nFor species identification \nFor screening \n \n \nFig 6.1. Thick and Thin Blood Smears.  \n(Lecturer\u2019s Slides)\u200b  \n \n \nFig 6.2 Thick (R) and Thin (L) Blood Smears Microscopic View \n(Lecturer\u2019s Slides)\u200b  \n \n6.2. QUANTITATIVE BUFFY COAT \n\u25cf\u200b\nThis test makes use of a capillary tube coated with \nanticoagulants and acridine orange for detection using a \nfluorescence microscope. \n\u25cb\u200b\nAcridine Orange is used to stain the malarial parasites \nto allow visualization of the parasite \n\u25cb\u200b\nWhen the fluorescent light is used, it excites the parasite \nthat will fluoresce against the dark background. \n\u25cb\u200b\nIf the results are positive, species identification must be \nconfirmed through a blood smear.  \n\u25cf\u200b\nFluorescence indicates the presence of the parasite. \n\u25cf\u200b\nResults are available in 8 minutes \n\u25cb\u200b\nShorter turnaround time than thick and thin smears \n\u25cf\u200b\nUses the principle of centrifugation and fluorescence that \nwould improve review by concentrating easily visible parasites \nin specific areas of the tube.  \n\u25cb\u200b\nBuffy Coat: areas where parasites are located \n \n \nFig 6.3 Quantitative Buffy Coat \n(Lecturer\u2019s Slides)\u200b  \n \n \nFig 6.4 Fluorescent Microscope viewing capillary tube \n(Lecturer\u2019s Slides)\u200b  \n \n \nFig 6.5 Positive and Negative Results under Fluorescent Microscope \nPositive shows presence of smaller dots that would fluoresce on the red \ncell layer and the big dots that fluoresce are the white cells \n(Lecturer\u2019s Slides)\u200b  \n \n6.3. MALARIA RAPID DIAGNOSTIC TESTS \n\u25cf\u200b\nImmunochromatographic method to detect specific malarial \nantigens \n\u25cf\u200b\nBlood sample from a needle prick is obtained \n\u25cb\u200b\nThen, a buffer is placed. Because of the lateral flow \nassay, results can be visualized (the control and test line) \n\u25cb\u200b\nThe control area must always have a line to indicate the \ntest is valid. Absence of line may indicate the test kit is \ndefective. \n\u25cf\u200b\nThe pads are coated with antibodies to detect antigens \npresence in the patient\u2019s sample. \n\u25cf\u200b\nPositive: Presence of red line at the test and control area \n\u25cf\u200b\nNegative: Presence of only control line \n\u25cf\u200b\nDifferent kits available \n\u25cb\u200b\nSome kits can only test for one antigen and some can \ncheck for more. \n\u25cb\u200b\nSome kits are already combined. There is already PV (P. \nvivax), PF (P. falciparum), PM (P. malariae). (Doc \nWalinsundin, 2024) \n\u25cf\u200b\nExamples of what is being detected by the RDTs: \n\u25cb\u200b\nP. falciparum histidine-rich protein 2 (PfHRP2) \n\u25cb\u200b\nPanmalarial antigen (PMA) \n\u25cb\u200b\nPlasmodium lactate dehydrogenase (pLDH) \n \n \n \nLet's make learning un-bear-ably fun \ud83d\udc3c  |  9 \n", "PARASITOLOGY | PLASMODIA \n \nFig 6.6 Malaria RDT \n(Lecturer\u2019s Slides)\u200b  \n \n6.4. OTHER DIAGNOSTIC TESTS \n\u25cf\u200b\nSerologic test: \n\u25cb\u200b\nIndirect Hemagglutination Assay (IHA) \n\u25cb\u200b\nEnzyme Linked Immunosorbent Assay (ELISA) \n\u25cf\u200b\nMolecular \n\u25cb\u200b\nPCR \n \n7. TREATMENT \n\u25cf\u200b\nArtemether-Lumefantrine \n\u25cf\u200b\nQuinine in combination with doxycycline tetracycline, or \n\u25cf\u200b\nclindamycin \n\u25cf\u200b\nArtesunate suppository \n\u25cf\u200b\nArtemisinin-combination based therapy \n \n8. PREVENTION & CONTROL \n\u25cf\u200b\nEarly diagnosis and treatment \n\u25cf\u200b\nVector control \n\u25cb\u200b\nUse of insecticides \n\u25cb\u200b\nDetection of breeding sites \n\u25cb\u200b\nEnvironmental manipulation \n\u25a0\u200b\nFumigation is not so effective as the mosquitoes can \nleave the fumigation then return after. \n\u25a0\u200b\nPlant \nplants \nthat \nhave \na \nstrong smell like \nlemongrass, eucalyptus, basil. \n\u25cb\u200b\nBiological control \n\u25cf\u200b\nUse of PPE \n\u25cb\u200b\nUse of long sleeves and light-colored shirts \n\u25cb\u200b\nUse of insect repellants \n\u25cf\u200b\nUse of insecticide-treated nets \n\u25cf\u200b\nUse of insecticide-treated paints \n\u25cf\u200b\nChemoprophylaxis \n\u25cb\u200b\nUsed before going to endemic areas \nX. INHERENT HOST FACTORS THAT OFFERS PROTECTION \nAGAINST MALARIAL INFECTION \n\u25cf\u200b\nG6PD deficient cells \n\u25cf\u200b\nHemoglobin S, C or E \n\u25cf\u200b\nThalassemia \n\u25cf\u200b\nOvalocytosis \n\u25cf\u200b\nDuffy blood group negative RBCs \n \n \n \n9. PLASMODIUM KNOWLESI \n \n9.1. EPIDEMIOLOGY \n\u25cf\u200b\n5th human malaria parasite \n\u25cf\u200b\nZoonotic: a simian parasite \n\u25cf\u200b\nFirst discovered in the blood of monkeys \n\u25cf\u200b\nDistribution: Southeast Asia \n \nPLASMODIUM KNOWLESI \nNatural Host \nLong-tailed macaque; pig-tailed \nmacaque; and leafy monkey \nAccidental Host \nMan \nPrincipal Vector \nAnopheles leucosphyrus \nMOT \n\u25cf\u200b\nMonkey \u2192 mosquito \u2192 man \n\u25cf\u200b\nBite from a mosquito that has \nfed from a macaque reservoir \nhost \n\u25cf\u200b\nBlood transfusion (?) \n\u25cf\u200b\nHuman \u2192 mosquito \u2192 \nhuman (experiments only) \n \n9.2. LIFE CYCLE \n\u25cf\u200b\nSimilar to P. falciparum \n\u25cf\u200b\nCompletes RBC cycle in 24hrs \n\u25cb\u200b\nThe patient experiences daily fever spikes \n\u25cf\u200b\nNo hypnozoite stage \n\u25cf\u200b\nNot yet known if P. knowlesi can be naturally transmitted from \nhuman to human via the mosquito without the intermediate \nhost (monkey) \n\u25cf\u200b\nMonkey \u2192 mosquito \u2192 man \n \n9.3. RISK FACTORS \n\u25cf\u200b\nOccupation (e.g. forest rangers) \n\u25cf\u200b\nMore predominant in males than females \n\u25cf\u200b\nAdults > children; >/=15 years old \n\u25cf\u200b\nSleeping outside \n\u25cf\u200b\nTravel to areas with known cases \n\u25cf\u200b\nPresence of wild monkeys \n\u25cf\u200b\nContact with monkeys in the last 4wks \n\u25cf\u200b\nLiving near forest \n\u25cf\u200b\nDeforestation \n\u25cf\u200b\nPresence of long grass around the house \n \n9.4. PROTECTIVE FACTORS \n\u25cf\u200b\nG6PD \n\u25cf\u200b\nUse of PPE \n\u25cf\u200b\nLives in high altitude \n\u25cb\u200b\nMosquitoes can only fly to a certain height \n\u25cf\u200b\nLives in a well-developed village \n\u25cf\u200b\nRice paddy fields around the house \n \n9.5. CLINICAL PRESENTATION \n\u25cf\u200b\nIncubation period: 8-12 days \n\u25cf\u200b\nClinical Presentation \n\u25cb\u200b\nAsymptomatic \n\u25cb\u200b\nSymptomatic \n\u25a0\u200b\nFever, chills, headache, myalgia, nausea, vomiting, \ncough, abdominal pain, tachypnea, splenomegaly, \nhepatomegaly, neurological symptoms (rare) \n \n9.6. LABORATORY DIAGNOSIS \n\u25cf\u200b\nCBC: thrombocytopenia, anemia, normal total WBC count \n\u25cf\u200b\nProlonged PT and PTT  \n\u25cf\u200b\nMost cases low parasitemia  \n\u25cf\u200b\nMild \nalteration \nin \ntransaminases, \nhyperbilirubinemia, \nhypoalbuminemia (in severe cases)  \n\u25cf\u200b\nAcute kidney injury, increased creatinine and hyponatremia in \nsevere cases) \n \n \n \n \nLet's make learning un-bear-ably fun \ud83d\udc3c  |  10 \n", "PARASITOLOGY | PLASMODIA \n9.6. METHODS OF DETECTION \n\u25cf\u200b\nNo specific method for this P. knowlesi \n\u25cf\u200b\nIn the ff. tests, what method should be used? Doc Dominguez \nsaid in her opinion, PCR is more specific since DNA material \nof this parasite is detected. \n\u25cf\u200b\nMicroscopy  \n\u25cb\u200b\nChallenging  \n\u25cb\u200b\nRing forms resemble those of P. falciparum \n\u25cb\u200b\nFormation of band forms similar to P. malariae  \n\u25cb\u200b\nAmoeboid forms similar to P vivax  \n\u25cf\u200b\nRapid diagnostic test (RDT) kits \n\u25cb\u200b\nLow detecting capability  \n\u25cb\u200b\nP. falciparum histidine-rich protein 2 (PfHRP2), plasmode \ndehydrogenase (pLDH) and plasmodial aldolase  \n\u25cf\u200b\nELISA  \n\u25cb\u200b\nP. falciparum and P. vivax recombinant antigens  \n\u25cf\u200b\nPCR \n\u25cb\u200b\nCostly, trained personnel  \n\u25cf\u200b\nLoop mediated isothermal amplification (LAMP)  \n\u25cb\u200b\nHigh sensitivity results to cross contamination which can \noccur easily \n \n9.7. MORPHOLOGY \n \n9.7.1 Trophozoite (Ring-form) \n \n \n9.7.2 Schizont \n \n \n9.7.3 Gametocyte \n \n9.8 TREATMENT \n\u25cf\u200b\nChloroquine \n\u25cf\u200b\nAtovaquone \n\u25cf\u200b\nProguanil \n\u25cf\u200b\nMefloquine \n\u25cf\u200b\nArtemisinins \n\u25cf\u200b\nQuinine \n\u25cf\u200b\nDoxycycline \n \n \n \nLet's make learning un-bear-ably fun \ud83d\udc3c  |  11 \n", "PARASITOLOGY | PLASMODIA \n9.9 PREVENTION AND CONTROL \n\u25cf\u200b\nUse of PPE \n\u25cb\u200b\nWhen going to forest, use long sleeves and apply \nmosquito repellant \n\u25cf\u200b\nLessen exposure or interaction with monkeys,  \n\u25cf\u200b\nReforestation - do not destroy the natural habitat of monkeys. \n\u25cf\u200b\nStrict planning of human dwellings and conservation areas \n\u25cf\u200b\nKeep residential areas dean \n\u25cf\u200b\nVector control \n\u25cf\u200b\nPlanned outdoor activities, locals and tourists \n\u25cf\u200b\nPublic education \n \n10. Q&A \n\u25cf\u200b\nWhat species of Plasmodium can cause malaria? \n\u25cb\u200b\nP. falciparum \n\u25cb\u200b\nP. vivax \n\u25cb\u200b\nP. malariae \n\u25cb\u200b\nP. ovale \n\u25cb\u200b\nP. knowlesi \n\u25cf\u200b\nVector of P. Falciparum is the male biting mosquito \n\u25cb\u200b\nFalse; must be female anopheles  \n\u25cf\u200b\nHumans serve as reservoir host of P. knowlesi malaria \n\u25cb\u200b\nFalse; monkeys \n\u25cf\u200b\nSporozoite is the infectious stage of malaria  \n\u25cb\u200b\nTrue \n\u25cf\u200b\nThick and thin smears are used to screen and identify \ninfective parasites  \n\u25cb\u200b\nTrue \n\u25cf\u200b\nPPE, chemoprophylaxis, and biological controls are some \napproaches to prevention \n\u25cb\u200b\nTrue \n\u25cf\u200b\nIdentify the parasite and its stage \n \n\u25cb\u200b\nP. falciparum  \n\u25cb\u200b\nLeft pic: Headphone structure \n\u25cb\u200b\nMiddle pic: Band type \n\u25cb\u200b\nRight pic: gametocyte  \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n\u25cf\u200b\nIdentify the parasite and its stage \n \n\u25cb\u200b\nP. ovale \n\u25cb\u200b\nLeft pic: Fimbriated edge   \n\u25cb\u200b\nRight pic: Schizonts \n \nTRANS CORRECTION FORM \nFor any corrections found in the final uploaded transes in the drive, \nkindly answer the GForms for Trans Corrections indicated below: \n \nLink: https://forms.gle/9UpzjQ8Ucy2KZC5Q8  \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \nLet's make learning un-bear-ably fun \ud83d\udc3c  |  12 \n", "PARASITOLOGY | PLASMODIA \nSUMMARY TABLE \n \n \nP. falciparum   \n P. vivax \nP. malariae \nP. ovale \nP. knowlesi \nDisease  \nMalignant Tertian  \nBenign Tertian  \nQuartan \nBenign Tertian \nQuotidian malaria \nAssociated \nwith  \nRecrudescence  \nRelapse  \nRecrudescence  \nRelapse \n- \nFebrile \nCycle  \n36-48 hours  \n48 hours  \n72 hours  \n48 hours \n- \nPreferred \n(Infected) \nRBC  \nYoung and old; not \nenlarged  \n \nAll stages of RBCs \nYounger  stages of \nRBCs \nOlder stages of \nRBCs \nYounger stages of \nRBCs \nYounger stages of \nRBCs \nMultiple \nInfection of \nRBC  \nDouble or triple can \nbe seen  \nNot seen  \nNot seen  \nMay be seen \nSeen \nRBC Size  \nNormal-sized  \nEnlarged (larger than \nnormal)  \nNormal-sized \nEnlarged (larger than \nnormal, oval or \nelongated in shape \nwith ragged edges)  \nNormal-sized \nStrippling  \n(+) Maurer\u2019s dots  \n(FM - Ferdinand \nMarcos) \n(+) Schuffner\u2019s dots  \n(VS - Vilma Santos) \n(+) Ziemann\u2019s dots  \n(MZ - Manila Zoo) \n(+) James\u2019 dots  \n(OJ - Orange Juice) \n(+) Sinton and \nMulligan\u2019s \nStages seen \nin PBS \nRing form and \ngametocyte; schizont \nRARE \nAll stages seen  \nAll stages seen  \nAll stages seen  \nAll stages seen  \n                          ASEXUAL (in Intermediate Host) \n \nSchizont \n\u25cf\u200b8 - 24 merozoites  \n\u25cf\u200bUsually \nsequestered in the \ninternal capillaries \n\u25cf\u200bRarely seen \n\u25cf\u200b12 - 24 merozoites  \n\u25cf\u200bAmoeboid-shape \n\u25cf\u200bPigmented \n\u25cf\u200bMay almost fill the \nRBC \n\u25cf\u200b6-12 \nmerozoites \nforming \nrosette \nwith a central mass \nof pigment \n\u25cf\u200bAlmost fills the cell \n\u25cf\u200b4-16 merozoites  \n\u25cf\u200bResembles \nP. \nmalariae \nbut \npigment is darker \n\u25cf\u200bCan \nbe \nlike P. \nvivax, but smaller \nand has lighter and \nless \ncoarse \npigments  \n\u25cf\u200bOval shape and \nfimbriation \n\u25cf\u200bFills \nthe \nentire \nRBC \n\u25cf\u200bPigmented \n\u25cf\u200bUp \nto \n16 \nmerozoites \n(average 10) \nTrophozoite \n(ring form) \n\u25cf\u200bVery small, 1/6 of \nRBC  \n\u25cf\u200bWith 1-2 nuclei  \n\u25cf\u200bMultiple infection of \nRBCs \n\u25cf\u200bApplique or accol\u00e9 \nform usually found \non the periphery of \nthe cell \n\u25cf\u200bOccupies \n1\u20443 \nof \nRBC  \n\u25cf\u200bRed dot with blue \ncytoplasmic ring \n\u25cf\u200bResembles \nP. \nvivax but thicker \ncytoplasm  \n\u25cf\u200bStretch across the \ndiameter of RBC \n(band form) with \ndark pigments \n \n \n \n \n \n \n\u25cf\u200bResembles \nP. \nvivax \nbut \nmore \ncompact \n\u25cf\u200bSingle or double \nchromatin dots \n\u25cf\u200bMultiple infection in \n1 RBC may be \nseen \n\u25cf\u200bMay have one or \ndouble \nchromatin \ndot \n\u25cf\u200bBand forms \n\u25cf\u200bMultiple infection of \nRBCs  \n \n \nLet's make learning un-bear-ably fun \ud83d\udc3c  |  13 \n", "PARASITOLOGY | PLASMODIA \n                               SEXUAL (in Final Host) \n \nGametocyte  \n\u25cf\u200bCrescent \nor \nbanana shaped \n\u25cf\u200bRound or oval  \n\u25cf\u200bLarge, may almost \nfill the RBC \n\u25cf\u200bPigmented  \n\u25cf\u200bMicro and macro \ngametocytes \n\u25cf\u200bLike P. vivax but \nsmaller  \n\u25cf\u200bLarge, \novoid, \noccupies \nnearly \nthe \nentire \nRBC, \npigmented \n\u25cf\u200bNearly \noccupy \nentire cell \n\u25cf\u200bScanty pigment \n\u25cf\u200bSpherical and fill \nthe RBC \n\u25cf\u200bPigmented \n \nP. falciparum \n \n\u25cf\u200b\nCauses the most serious disease \n\u25cf\u200b\nProduces heavy parasitemia \n\u25cf\u200b\nWith double rings and headphone forms \n\u25cf\u200b\nDoes \nnot \ncause \nenlargement \nof \ncytoplasm of the parasitized erythrocyte \n\u25cf\u200b\nInduce \nformation \nof \nlarge, purplish \nMaurer\u2019s dots \n \n \n \n \n\u25cf\u200b\nLater trophozoite stage on the right \n\u25cf\u200b\nThis stage is not usually observed on \nspecimens \nfrom \npatients \nwith \na \nP. \nfalciparum infection. \n\u25cf\u200b\nFrequently found in cases of infection with \nthe other \n\u25cf\u200b\nthree species of Plasmodium \n \n\u25cf\u200b\nBanana-shaped gametocytes seen only \nin infections due to this species. \n\u25cf\u200b\nPeripheral \nblood \nreveals \nonly \ngametocytes and ring forms in P. \nfalciparum infections. \n\u25cf\u200b\nAt the gametocyte stage, the red cell \nmembrane may be invisible \n \n\u25cf\u200b\nHeadphone structure is created by two \ndots of chromatin on the same ring \n \n\u25cf\u200b\nThick blood smear \n\u25cf\u200b\nMultiple ring forms are visible \n \n\u25cf\u200b\nThick blood smear \n\u25cf\u200b\nCrescent-shaped gametocyte displays \nprominent brown pigment \n \nP. vivax \n \n\u25cf\u200b\nThe infected red blood cells can be \nenlarged up to twice their normal size \nwhen infected by P. vivax or P. ovale. \nEosinophilic cytoplasmic stippling, called \nSchuffner\u2019s dots, are also present in \nerythrocytes infected with these two \nspecies. \n \n\u25cf\u200b\nWith Schuffner\u2019s dots around the edges \nand the typical heavy chromatin \n \n \n\u25cf\u200b\nLarge cell size \n\u25cf\u200b\nSchuffner\u2019s dots \n \n \n \nLet's make learning un-bear-ably fun \ud83d\udc3c  |  14 \n", "PARASITOLOGY | PLASMODIA \n \n\u25cf\u200b\nThick blood smear \n\u25cf\u200b\nMature schizonts \n\u25cf\u200b\nAverage of 16 merozoites (12-24) \n\u25cf\u200b\nPigment is usually golden-brown and not \nvery vprominent \n \n \n \nP. malariae \n \n\u25cf\u200b\nSchizont in a typical rosette formation \n\u25cf\u200b\nSchizont has 6-110 merozoites \n\u25cf\u200b\nHemozin pigment, brown in color \n\u25cf\u200b\nPigment, although seen in all 4 species, \nare most prominent in P. malariae \n \n\u25cf\u200b\nBand-form trophozoite \n\u25cf\u200b\nNo Schuffner\u2019s dots \n \n \nP. ovale \n \n\u25cf\u200b\nInfected erythrocytes may develop a \nfimbriated edge \n \n\u25cf\u200b\nThick Blood Smear \n\u25cf\u200b\nSchizonts of this species may develop up to 16 merozoites, they can reveal few \nnumbers during the early stages of development \n\u25cf\u200b\nHemazoin pigment is most difficult to see in schizonts of this species. \n \nP. knowlesi \nTrophozoite (Ring form) \nSchizont \nGametocyte \n \n \nLet's make learning un-bear-ably fun \ud83d\udc3c  |  15 \n", "PARASITOLOGY | PLASMODIA \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \nLet's make learning un-bear-ably fun \ud83d\udc3c  |  16 \n"]}];